window.EB_SB_ADD({"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-2-castle-grounds/agonizing-thorns":{"description":"<p>A massive tangle of brambles weaves through a skeleton clad in armor.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 27 Survival to delicately unthread the vines from the skeleton without triggering them; four successes are required to disable the haunt.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(expert)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"The Thorns Need Blood","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A person attempts to damage the vines or to Interact with the skeleton or its armor</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The thorny vines suddenly animate, and in so doing, make it appear that the skeleton is rising to attack. The vines lash out, and all creatures in area <strong>B2b</strong> or within 20 feet above take 8d6 slashing damage from the thorns (DC 29 basic Reflex save). A creature that fails the save also takes 1d6 persistent bleed damage (2d6 persistent bleed damage on a critical failure).</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-4-the-reception-halls/alisendra-arudora":{"lang":["Aklo","Chthonian","Common","Diabolic","Varisian"],"gear":["Alisendra's Fan"],"abilities":[{"n":"Bloodsense (Imprecise) 90 feet","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A blood hag can sense the presence of blood and creatures with blood. They can tell the difference between spilled blood and the blood within a living creature.</p>"},{"n":"Borrowed Skin","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Alisandra's \"borrowed skin\" is her own skin from her previous life.</p><hr><p>A blood hag wears a covering of skin stolen from a humanoid creature they've killed, hiding their true form. They appear as the creature whose skin they're wearing, including to spells that would detect that creature. Creatures can still potentially detect the deception as described in the Impersonate action.</p>\n<p>Spreading coarse salt inside the skin prevents the hag from putting it back on, forcing them to keep their fiery form until they kill another humanoid and spend 1 hour turning it into a new disguise.</p>"},{"n":"Coven","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>Although Alisendra is on her own, the ambient energies of the Bastardhall Curse bolster her as if she were a part of a coven. She adds Nightmare, Outcast's Curse, and Scrying to her innate spells.</p>"},{"n":"Assume Fiery Form","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","fire","occult","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>The blood hag removes their borrowed skin and transforms into a brilliant ball of fire. They become amorphous (allowing them to move through a gap at least 1 foot wide without Squeezing and move at full Speed while Squeezing), gain the fire trait and a fly Speed of 60 feet, become immune to fire, and emit light as a torch. They lose their melee Strikes and can't Drain Blood, but they deal 4d6 fire damage with a DC 29 basic Reflex save to each creature that touches them, including a creature that hits them with an unarmed attack or a weapon attack from an adjacent space. If their skin is intact and they're adjacent to it, they can Interact to return to their normal form inside the skin.</p>\n<p>The hag can choose to Assume Fiery Form as a single action, bursting through their skin in a blast of flames. Doing so destroys their borrowed skin and deals 9d6 fire damage to all creatures in a 20-foot emanation with a DC 26 basic Reflex save.</p>\n<p>Effect: Fiery Form</p>"},{"n":"Drain Blood","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> A Grabbed, Paralyzed, Restrained, Unconscious, or willing creature is within the blood hag's reach</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The hag sinks their fangs into the creature to drink its blood. This requires a successful Athletics vs. Fortitude DC check against the victim's Fortitude DC if the victim is grabbed and is automatic for any of the other conditions. The victim becomes Drained 1. The hag regains 15 healing Hit Points, gaining any excess HP as temporary Hit Points that last for 1 hour. Drinking blood from a creature that's already drained doesn't restore any Hit Points to the hag but increases the victim's drained value by 1, killing the victim when it reaches drained 5.</p>\n<p>A victim's drained condition decreases by 1 per week. A blood transfusion, which requires a successful DC 20 Medicine check and sufficient blood or a blood donor, reduces the drained condition by 1 after 10 minutes.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-7-the-fallen-temple/amondi":{"lang":["Common"],"abilities":[{"n":"Site Bound","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>These three ghosts can only leave the crypts when Bastardhall appears on Golarion, and even then, they can't leave the island.</p>"},{"n":"Rejuvenation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>The three adventurers re-form after 2d4 days here in the crypts unless someone carries their respective urn of ashes down to the temple in Maiserene (area <strong>A9</strong>) to be cleansed by Shirail.</p>"},{"n":"Amondi's Cruel Shiv","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","move"],"f":"","d":"<p>Amondi Flies once, moving through a target creature's space; this movement doesn't trigger reactions. As she moves through the target's space, she makes a ghostly weapon Strike against the target. If she hits, she deals an additional 3d6 persistent bleed damage, and the creature struck becomes Sickened 1 from the pain of the bloody wound.</p>"},{"n":"Malevolent Possession","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["incapacitation","mental","possession"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ghost attempts to possess an adjacent corporeal creature. This has the same effect as the Possession spell (DC 23 Will), except since the ghost doesn't have a physical body, it is unaffected by that restriction of the spell.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-4-the-reception-halls/amyleda-blackwell":{"lang":["Chthonian","Common","Diabolic","Draconic","Empyrean","Necril","Varisian"],"gear":["Bladed Scarf","Harrow Deck (Exquisite)"],"notes":{"saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. magic"},"abilities":[{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Magic","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Rejection Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>As succubi are beings of pure lust, creatures that reject their lust can metaphysically harm them. When a succubus fails a Diplomacy check to Embrace or Request, or when a creature succeeds at its save against a succubus's mental spell or ability, the succubus takes 2d8 mental damage.</p>\n<p>For 1 hour after causing mental damage to a succubus in this way, a creature can deal 2d8 mental damage to the succubus with a successful Demoralize action incorporating its rejection.</p>"},{"n":"Seductive Presence","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","emotion","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>10 feet.</p>\n<p>Any creature in the aura that could be sexually attracted to a succubus takes a –2 circumstance penalty to checks and DCs to oppose the succubus's mental spells, Deception, and Diplomacy.</p>"},{"n":"Change Shape","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","divine","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>The succubus can take on the appearance of any Small or Medium humanoid. This doesn't change their Speed or their attack and damage modifiers with their Strikes, but it might change the damage type their Strikes deal (typically to bludgeoning).</p>\n<hr>\n<p><p>The monster changes its shape indefinitely. It can use this action again to return to its natural shape or adopt a new shape. Unless otherwise noted, a monster cannot use Change Shape to appear as a specific individual. Using Change Shape counts as creating a disguise for the Impersonate use of Deception. The monster's transformation automatically defeats Perception DCs to determine whether the creature is a member of the ancestry or creature type into which it transformed, and it gains a +4 status bonus to its Deception DC to prevent others from seeing through its disguise. Change Shape abilities specify what shapes the monster can adopt. The monster doesn't gain any special abilities of the new shape, only its physical form. For example, in each shape, it replaces its normal Speeds and Strikes, and might potentially change its senses or size. Any changes are listed in its stat block.</p></p>"},{"n":"Embrace","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["attack"],"f":"","d":"<p>The succubus attempts to Grapple a creature using their Diplomacy bonus instead of Athletics. If the creature is willing, the succubus automatically succeeds.</p>"},{"n":"Passionate Kiss","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["divine","emotion","mental","unholy","void"],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The succubus engages a creature they have Grabbed or Restrained in an embrace or other act of passion to drain its vital essence.</p>\n<p>The kiss makes the creature Drained 1 or increases its drained value by 1, to a maximum of 4. The creature takes 3d6 void damage and the succubus regains Hit Points equal to the damage dealt. The target must succeed at a DC 28 Will save or be affected by a Suggestion to submit to more actions of passion rather than trying to Escape.</p>"},{"n":"Profane Gift","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["divine","mental"],"f":"once per day","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per day</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The succubus gives a willing humanoid a profane gift. That creature gains a +1 status bonus to attack rolls, skill checks, and saving throws. As long as the gift persists, the succubus can communicate telepathically with the target at any distance, see through the creature's senses, and target the creature with suggestion through the telepathic link. In addition, the creature uses an outcome one degree of success worse than it rolls on saving throws against the succubus's Suggestion spells.</p>\n<p>A humanoid can't have more than one profane gift at a time, and a succubus can't grant more than one profane gift at a time. Removing the gift requires an atone ritual. The succubus can remove the gift as a free action to give the recipient a curse, making them Stupefied 3 with an unlimited duration.</p>\n<p>A summoned succubus can't grant a profane gift.</p>\n<p>Effect: Profane Gift</p>"},{"n":"Spiritual Anguish","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","emotion","mental","unholy"],"f":"","d":"<p>Amyleda gives a creature within 30 feet a wistful smile, then instills within their mind the sudden conviction that someone who loved them in the past has either betrayed them or died. The targeted creature takes 8d6 mental damage and must attempt a DC 28 Will save. Amyleda can't use Spiritual Anguish again for 1 hour or until she gives a creature a Passionate Kiss—whichever comes first.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected and is temporarily immune for 24 hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and is Stupefied 1 by the distraction for 1 minute.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes double damage and is stupefied 1 from the distraction for 24 hours.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/adventure-toolbox/anophilex":{"lang":["Chthonian","Common","Diabolic","Empyrean","Requian","telepathy 100 feet"],"flavor":"<p>The sight of blood is frightening proof of another's suffering, and when that blood is one's own, that fear can turn to blinding terror. Such are the horrors that the skittering, constantly bleeding anophilex sahkil revels in. With a glistening, spiderlike body covered in pulsing sacs of blood caught amid tangles of thorny vines, an anophilex's head is a freshly skinned skull with sharp fangs and patches of long black hair pulled back to tangle amid the thorns of their bodies. As an anophilex moves, their thorns rip and tear at their bloody blisters and bloated organs, yet the bleeding only flows back along their body to form new thorny lengths in a constant recreation that gives off an unsettling, gurgling susurrus. An anophilex has little patience for subtlety or manipulation, preferring to skitter through or flap above battlefields, among refugees, or amid any area where the wounded gather, so they can spread fear and drink the wounded's crimson pain.</p><h2>About Sahkils</h2><p>Sahkils are fiends associated with primal fears and phobic terrors that haunt mortal minds. They hail from the depths of the Ethereal Plane and serve sinister demigods known collectively as tormentors, such as Charg himself. For more information about sahkils, see page 274 of Pathfinder Monster Core 2.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Easy to Call","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>An anophilex's level is considered 2 lower for the purpose of being conjured by the Binding Circle ritual (and potentially other rituals, at the GM's discretion), but it is always free to attack or leave instead of negotiate unless the primary caster's check is a critical success.</p>"},{"n":"Fearful Bleeding","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["auditory","aura","divine","emotion","fear","mental","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. The sight and sound of the anophilex's constantly bleeding body fills non-sahkils with fears that their own blood wants to flow and surge from their veins as well. Creatures taking persistent bleed damage become Frightened 1 when they enter the aura or start their turn in the aura.</p>"},{"n":"Drain Blood","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When the anophilex uses a jaws strike against a creature that's taking persistent bleed damage, they drink the blood. The creature must succeed at a DC 30 Fortitude save or become Enfeebled 1 (Enfeebled 2 on a critical failure). The anophilex becomes Quickened on the following round, and can use the extra action to Stride or Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Skip Between","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["divine","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p>The sahkil moves from the Universe to the Ethereal Plane or vice-versa. While in the Ethereal Plane, they're unable to affect anything in the Universe, and they cannot be seen by beings or abilities in the Universe, unless these have an ability that can gaze into the Ethereal Plane. While on the Material Plane the inverse is true for anything on the Ethereal Plane. A summoned sahkil can't use Skip Between.</p>"},{"n":"Spray Vile Blood","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>The anophilex twists their body into a knot, causing their thorns to pierce their body so that unholy blood sprays out in a 30-foot emanation. Non-sahkil creatures in this area take 12d6 spirit damage (DC 30 basic Will save). The anophilex can't Spray Vile Blood again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/adventure-toolbox/aron-mordimus":{"lang":["Common"],"notes":{"saves":"–1 status penalty to all saves vs. death effects"},"abilities":[{"n":"Bound to Bastardhall","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Unlike the typical phantom, Aron exists fully in the Ethereal Plane and cannot shift back and forth between this plane and the Universe.</p>"},{"n":"–1 Status Penalty to All Saves vs. Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Bound Even in Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>As phantom, but if Aron is reduced to 0 Hit Points by any means, his soul does not return to the River of Souls. Instead, his soul is drawn into the soul vortex in the vault under Bastardhall (area <strong>I</strong>).</p>"},{"n":"Physical Manifestation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Upon reaching rank 1, Aron manifests a semi-physical body. He loses the incorporeal trait, his immunities and resistances, and his fly Speed, and gains a land Speed of 25 feet. He has a Strength modifier of +0, an Athletics modifier of +7, an AC of 21, 80 HP, and Pour Elixir. His other statistics do not change.</p>"},{"n":"Phantom Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["spirit"],"f":"","d":"<p>Each time they make a Strike, a phantom can choose to deal spirit damage instead of the normal physical damage type.</p>"},{"n":"Pour Elixir","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate","occult"],"f":"twice per day","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> Aron has a physical body</p>\n<p><strong>Frequency</strong> twice per day</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Aron forms a pitcher in one hand from ectoplasm, then pours liquid from the pitcher out onto a solid surface. As the liquid pours, it forms into a single glass vial filled with an alchemical elixir of Aron's choice. He's always willing and eager to Pour an Elixir for the PCs when asked. An elixir created by this ability vanishes if not used within 24 hours of being poured. He can choose from the following options when he uses Pour Elixir: Moderate Antidote, Moderate Antiplague, Moderate Darkvision Elixir, Moderate Eagle Eye Elixir, or Lesser Elixir of Life.</p>\n<p><strong>Rank 2</strong> Aron's elixirs are now greater versions rather than moderate (or in the case of an elixir of life, a moderate one instead of a lesser one).</p>\n<p><strong>Rank 3</strong> Aron can now Pour Elixir three times a day, and his elixirs of life are now greater elixirs of life.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-4-the-reception-halls/arth-wiltur":{"lang":["Common"],"lore":[["Hunting Lore",15],["Sailing Lore",15]],"gear":["Oar","Fish Hook"],"notes":{"saves":"–2 status penalty to all saves vs. curses"},"abilities":[{"n":"–2 Status Penalty to All Saves vs. Curses","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Hooked","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature struck by Arth's fish hook is skewered, taking 1d4 persistent bleed damage as long as the hook remains stuck. A creature can Escape from the hook.</p>"},{"n":"Humiliate the Fish","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> A creature is hooked by Arth's fish hook and adjacent to Arth</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Arth yanks the hook free, causing 4d8 slashing damage to the creature (DC 28 basic Fortitude save), who also falls Prone if they fail that Fortitude save.</p>"},{"n":"Reel In","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate"],"f":"","d":"<p>Arth pulls the rope attached to his hook. If he has no creature hooked, the fish hook returns to his hand. If a creature is hooked, Arth attempts an Athletics vs. Fortitude DC check against the creature's Fortitude DC, pulling it up to 30 feet closer on a success (60 feet on a critical success).</p>"},{"n":"Swear","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","curse","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>Arth unleashes a string of invectives. Each creature that hears him must attempt a DC 28 Will save, with the effects of Mariner's Curse, and is then temporarily immune for 24 hours. Even on a failed save, the curse ends automatically after 24 hours.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/augsten-arudora":{"lang":["Common"],"gear":["Whip","Augsten's Cudgel"],"abilities":[{"n":"Bully the Bully","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["auditory","concentrate","emotion","linguistic","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>Augsten's sense of strength and self-worth shatters whenever someone calls him \"Little Lord Augie.\" A PC can attempt to send Augsten into a tantrum by calling him by this insult as part of a special attempt to Create a diversion that has the auditory and linguistic traits, with the following results.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> Augsten is cut deep by your teasing! He becomes Stunned 1 by his rage, and is Off-Guard to your attacks for the next 24 hours. During this time, he takes a –4 circumstance penalty to all attempts to use Demoralize on any target, and can't use Bullying Command.</p>\n<p>Effect: Bully the Bully (Critical Success)</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> Your insult enrages Augsten, causing him to become off-guard to your attacks until the start of his next turn as he's distracted by his anger. The next attempt to insult him using this by any PC gains a +2 circumstance bonus to the Deception check.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> Your attempt fails to rile Augsten up, but it does attract his attention. He focuses his attacks on you on his next turn.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> Your attempt to tease Augsten backfires, and instead of sending him into a sputtering tantrum, you enrage him. He gains a +2 status bonus to all damage rolls against you for the next 24 hours, during which time you cannot attempt to Bully the Bully. Augsten favors you as his target for all attacks during this time.</p>\n<p>Effect: Bully the Bully (Critical Failure)</p>"},{"n":"Reactive Bullying","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature Augsten can see fails an attack roll or skill check</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Augsten attempts to Demoralize the triggering creature by taunting it with cruel laughter and insults about its ability.</p>"},{"n":"Bullying Command","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","emotion","fear","linguistic","mental"],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Augsten bellows out an insulting, bullying order to a bugbear tormentor he can see. That bugbear tormentor becomes Quickened until the end of their next turn, and can use the extra action to Interact, Step, Stride, or Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Cruel Lashes","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When Augsten wields a whip, the whip loses its nonlethal trait. He deals an additional 1d6 precision damage with a whip against frightened foes.</p>"},{"n":"Cudgeling Blow","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate","primal"],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> Augsten is carrying his cudgel in one hand</p>\n<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Augsten smashes his cudgel against an adjacent creature with astonishing force, dealing 3d8 bludgeoning damage and forcing the target to attempt a DC 26 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature takes no damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and is Off-Guard until the start of its next turn.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes double damage, is knocked Prone, and is off-guard until the start of its next turn.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/adventure-toolbox/ausken-dast":{"notes":{"saves":"–1 status penalty to all saves vs. death effects"},"abilities":[{"n":"Bound to Bastardhall","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Unlike the typical phantom, Ausken exists fully in the Ethereal Plane and cannot shift back and forth between this plane and the Universe.</p>"},{"n":"–1 Status Penalty to All Saves vs. Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Bound Even in Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>As phantom, but if Ausken is reduced to 0 Hit Points by any means, his soul does not return to the River of Souls. Instead, his soul is drawn into the soul vortex in the vault under Bastardhall (area <strong>I</strong>).</p>"},{"n":"Physical Manifestation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Upon reaching rank 1, Ausken manifests a semi-physical body. He loses the incorporeal trait, his immunities and resistances, and his fly Speed, and gains a land Speed of 25 feet. He has a Strength modifier of +5, an Athletics modifier of +12, an AC of 22, 78 HP, and can use Sheriff's Blessing. His other statistics do not change.</p>"},{"n":"Phantom Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["spirit"],"f":"","d":"<p>Each time they make a Strike, a phantom can choose to deal spirit damage instead of the normal physical damage type.</p>"},{"n":"Sheriff's Blessing","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"once per day","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> Ausken has a physical body</p>\n<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per day</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Ausken touches a willing character and infuses their mind and spirit with bravery. Until the next sunset, the touched character gains a +1 status bonus to all saving throws against fear effects, and any time the character gains the frightened condition, reduce its value by 1. Ausken is always willing and eager to give his blessing to a PC when asked.</p>\n<p><strong>Rank 2</strong> The blessed character now treats any success on a saving throw against a fear effect as a critical success instead.</p>\n<p><strong>Rank 3</strong> Ausken can touch two willing characters to give both of them his blessing, and the effects of the blessing now last for 24 hours.</p>\n<p>Effect: Sheriff's Blessing</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-8-the-vault/aznithakantus":{"lang":["Common","Necril","truespeech"],"lore":[["Bardic Lore",27]],"notes":{"hp":"void healing","saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. vitality"},"abilities":[{"n":"Bardic Lore","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Sphinxes are naturally curious, and their love of puzzles and mysteries leads them to gather information on a broad range of topics. Sphinxes have Bardic Lore, a special Lore skill that can be used only to Recall Knowledge, but on any topic.</p>"},{"n":"Rejuvenation","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>When Aznithakantus is destroyed, the energies of the Bastardhall Curse rebuild her body on the steps of the onyx ziggurat over 1d10 days; if her body is destroyed during that time, the process starts as new. She can be destroyed permanently if the Bastardhall Curse is lifted.</p>"},{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Vitality","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Blindingly Flammable","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","light","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. Aznithakantus's leathery undead flesh is infused with strange reagents. Whenever she takes fire damage, the fire flares blindingly bright, and any creature in the aura's area must succeed at a DC 30 Fortitude save or become Dazzled for 1 round (or Blinded for 1 minute on a critical failure); this is a visual effect. When she takes persistent fire damage, these flames leap out and burn any creature in the aura for an equal amount of damage (DC 30 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Claw Rake","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The sphinx rears back on their hind legs and makes two claw Strikes at the same target, using the same attack bonus as their highest melee attack. If both attacks deal damage, the target takes extra damage equal to one claw Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Gift of Dust","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["occult","void"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> Aznithakantus's last action was a successful claw Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Dust from Aznithakantus's claw seeps into the wound, infecting the target with painful aches as the flesh around the wound grows gray and begins to flake away in dusty chunks. The creature takes 5d8 void damage with a DC 24 basic Will save. If the creature critically fails the saving throw, it becomes Stunned 3.</p>"},{"n":"Pounce","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The sphinx Strides and makes a Strike at the end of that movement. If the sphinx began this action Hidden, they remain hidden until after the attack.</p>"},{"n":"Riddler's Rune","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Once per week, a Sphinx can create a magical symbol as the Rune Trap ritual. The sphinx usually shapes it to take the form of a written riddle and sets the password to the answer.</p>\n<p>A creature that gives the wrong answer or tries to pass without answering must succeed at a DC 33 Will save or be affected by one of the following spells, chosen by the sphinx when creating the symbol: Synaptic Pulse (7th), Charm (7th), Fear (7th), Phantom Pain (7th), Sleep (7th). The sphinx learns the identity of any creature that answers the riddle and tends to be friendly to them if they answered correctly.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/adventure-toolbox/banijer-greely":{"lang":["Common"],"notes":{"saves":"–1 status penalty to all saves vs. death effects"},"abilities":[{"n":"Bound to Bastardhall","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Unlike the typical phantom, Banijer exists fully in the Ethereal Plane and cannot shift back and forth between this plane and the Universe.</p>"},{"n":"–1 Status Penalty to All Saves vs. Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Bound Even in Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>As phantom, but if Banijer is reduced to 0 Hit Points by any means, his soul does not return to the River of Souls. Instead, his soul is drawn into the soul vortex in the vault under Bastardhall (area <strong>I</strong>).</p>"},{"n":"Physical Manifestation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Upon reaching rank 1, Banijer manifests a semi-physical body. He loses the incorporeal trait, his immunities and resistances, and his fly Speed, and gains a land Speed of 25 feet. He has a Strength modifier of +1, an Athletics modifier of +9, an AC of 21, 73 HP, and primal innate spells. His other statistics do not change.</p>"},{"n":"Phantom Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["spirit"],"f":"","d":"<p>Each time they make a Strike, a phantom can choose to deal spirit damage instead of the normal physical damage type.</p>"},{"n":"Spellcasting","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Once Banijer is rank 1 and has a physical body, he gains the following primal innate spells. As his rank increases, his primal spell DC and spell attack modifier increase, and he gains additional innate spells.</p>\n<p><strong>Primal Innate Spells</strong> DC 22, attack +14; <strong>3rd</strong> Cleanse Affliction, Heal; <strong>2nd</strong> Cleanse Affliction, Heal, Sure Footing; <strong>1st</strong> Cleanse Cuisine, Heal, Mending; <strong>Cantrips (3rd)</strong> Caustic Blast, Detect Magic, Light, Read Aura, Tangle Vine</p>\n<p><strong>Rank 2</strong> DC 24, attack +16; <strong>4th</strong> Cleanse Affliction; <strong>3rd</strong> Water Breathing.</p>\n<p>His cantrips are heightened to 4th rank.</p>\n<p><strong>Rank 3</strong> DC 26, attack +18; <strong>5th</strong> Sure Footing; <strong>4th</strong> Heal.</p>\n<p>His cantrips are heightened to 5th rank.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-1-the-haunted-shore/bastardhall-rat":{"flavor":"<p>Giant rats are enormous versions of the common vermin. They are typically found in abundant numbers, but since they cannot fit in the nooks where mundane rats typically hide, they are much easier to locate and exterminate. They mostly live in sewers where they can scavenge from the streets above, but some families of giant rats live in more remote locations, such as dank caves, forests or hills. Rats are incredibly adept survivors and can be found nearly anywhere in the world, though they tend to favor temperate or warm climates as opposed to cold regions.</p>\n<p>Although its bite alone is not lethal except to the very young or very old, the giant rat carries the putrid plague common to rodents around the world—a pestilence more than capable of ravaging rural communities.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Rats are a ubiquitous menace, scurrying through the sewers and on the streets of nearly every settlement in the world. Though a regular rat darting underfoot might startle or even frighten the average passerby, giant rats and rat swarms are far more dangerous.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Ratpox","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["curse"],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature that takes damage from a Bastardhall rat's bite is exposed to a painful and debilitating curse, and must attempt a DC 22 Fortitude save against the following effects.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected by the curse.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> Pain shoots through the creature as angry redringed blisters form in the vicinity of the bite. The creature becomes Sickened 1 by the pain.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> As success, but Sickened 2. In addition, the blisters linger even after the initial pain abates, leaving the creature Enfeebled 1 from the discomfort for as long as the curse persists. Each time the sun rises, the creature can attempt a DC 22 Fortitude save to end the curse's effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the curse has an unlimited duration, and its effects grow more discomforting after dark, increasing to Enfeebled 2 and Clumsy 1 while the sun is down.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/blasphemous-blood":{"description":"<p>The walls and ceiling begin bleeding more and more, and gouts of cursed blood spew from both decapitated statues' necks.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 23 Religion to exorcise the influence of Charg from a statue, DC 26 Thievery to dismantle the magical blood generator in a statue's neck, Dispel Magic (3rd-rank, counteract DC 20) to render a statue inert; each statue must be disabled separately</p>","reset":"<p>The blasphemous blood deactivates at the start of any round in which no creatures other than a sahkil are in area <strong>C13</strong>, then resets after 3 rounds.</p>","routine":"<p>(2 actions) The hazard sprays a sizzling gout of cursed blood from the neck stumps of each of the defiled statues of Pharasma for each of its actions. If a statue is disabled or destroyed, the hazard loses that corresponding action. Each gout targets a randomly determined non-sahkil creature in area <strong>C13</strong>, who must then attempt a DC 22 Reflex save against the following effects. After dark, this trap gains 4 actions instead of 2, with each statue firing two gouts of blood. Regardless of how many gouts are fired, a creature can be targeted only once per round by these gouts.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target takes 2d6 spirit damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target takes 2d8 spirit damage, 1d6 persistent bleed damage, and is exposed to the curse of death's blood.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The target takes 4d8 spirit damage, 2d6 persistent bleed damage, and is exposed to the curse of death's blood (and takes a –2 circumstance penalty to save against the curse).</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(expert)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Let the Walls Weep Blood","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A non-sahkil creature moves more than 10 feet into area <strong>C13</strong></p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The walls and ceiling begin to weep blood at an alarming rate, causing the floor of area <strong>C13</strong> to grow slick. A non-sahkil that moves across the slick floor is Off-Guard and must Balance (DC 20 Acrobatics) as long as the hazard remains active. An area of effect with the cold trait created in this area freezes the blood on the floor, making it even more slippery (increasing the Acrobatics check to Balance to DC 22). The hazard then rolls initiative.</p>"},{"n":"Curse of Death's Blood","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["curse","divine"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 22 Fortitude</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Your blood grows cold and imbued with death. Whenever you are struck by a critical hit, you take an additional 1d6 persistent bleed damage, and as long as you are taking persistent bleed damage, you become Enfeebled 1 as your body weakens under the lure of death's embrace. If you make your daily preparations at a location sanctified to Pharasma or Shelyn (such as area <strong>A9</strong> after Shirail's phantom returns), you can attempt a new saving throw to end this curse.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/brensen-firecask":{"lang":["Common","Varisian"],"lore":[["Alcohol Lore",17]],"gear":["Glaive","Red Cap","Soulheart","Iron Boots","Bottle of Wine","Gold Engagement Ring"],"notes":{"hp":"fast healing 10"},"abilities":[{"n":"Red Cap","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>A redcap's woolen hat is dyed with the blood of their victims. If the redcap loses their cap, they no longer benefit from fast healing and take a –4 status penalty to their damage rolls. They can create a new cap in 10 minutes, but that cap doesn't grant them powers until the redcap has turned it red with Blood Soak. A cap has no benefit for creatures other than the redcap who made it.</p>\n<p>Effect: Lost Red Cap</p>"},{"n":"Fast Healing 10","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A monster with this ability regains the given number of Hit Points each round at the beginning of its turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Bolster Life","a":"2","c":"defensive","t":["concentrate","manipulate"],"f":"once per day","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per day</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> When held to the chest and focused upon, the soul energy within the soulheart floods outward and bolsters your life force, granting you 6 temporary Hit Points (or 3 temporary Hit Points if it's nighttime). These temporary Hit Points last for 8 hours.</p>"},{"n":"Divine Revulsion","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>If a redcap sees a creature brandish a religious symbol of a deity (which requires an Interact action by that creature) or cast a divine spell while wearing a religious symbol, the redcap must attempt a DC 24 Will save. The DC is DC 26 Will against a symbol of Shelyn and DC 34 Will against his lucky dawnsilver corkscrew.</p>\n<p>They then become temporarily immune to all brandished religious symbols for 10 minutes.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The redcap is unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The redcap is Frightened 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The redcap gains the Fleeing condition for 1 round and is Frightened 4.</p>"},{"n":"Sommelier's Mockery","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["mental"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature Brensen can see within 30 feet spends an action retching in an attempt to recover from the sickened condition and fails</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Brensen points and laughs at the triggering creature, causing their sickened condition value to increase by 1, or to fall Prone if their sickened condition value is already Sickened 3.</p>"},{"n":"Soak Cap","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate"],"f":"","d":"<p>Brensen dips his cap into red wine or blood from a slain foe. Wine must have been opened by Brensen within the last minute. Blood must be from a creature Brensen helped kill and must have died within the last minute. He gains a +4 status bonus to damage rolls and Fortitude saves for 1 minute.</p>\n<p>Effect: Blood Soak</p>"},{"n":"Sommelier's Trick","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate","mental","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>Brensen takes a deep gulp of alcohol, then winks at a creature he can see within 30 feet. The alcohol he imbibed suddenly infuses the belly of the target, causing them to attempt a DC 25 Fortitude save with the following effects.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature becomes Sickened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature becomes Sickened 2 and falls Prone.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the creature becomes Slowed 1 as long as it's remain sickened.</p>"},{"n":"Stomp","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The redcap Strides up to half its Speed and makes a boot Strike at any point during that movement. If the boot Strike hits a Prone creature, it deals an extra 2d6 bleed.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-1-the-haunted-shore/bring-dusk-to-dawn":{"description":"<p>Shadows rise up from the empty tub, creeping along the floor, walls, and celling to blot out light.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 25 Religion or DC 25 Nature (expert) to burn out the shadowy influence through prayer or meditation, DC 28 Arcana or DC 28 Occultism (trained) to use magic to block the shadowy influence from being able to manifest at all; three successes are required to disable the haunt.</p>","reset":"<p>The haunt deactivates at the end of any round in which no living creature is in area <strong>A19</strong>, then resets 1 round later.</p>","routine":"<p>(2 actions) A shadowy figure rises up from the bath, resembling the upper torso of a demonic humanoid with glowing eyes. On its first action, the haunt sweeps its many arms through area <strong>A19</strong>, dealing 4d6 void damage on all living creatures in the area (DC 21 basic Fortitude save). On its second action, it either spawns an undead shadow from its mass, or it Sustains a currently spawned undead shadow; it can only have one shadow active at a time. This shadow has the minion trait, cannot leave area <strong>A19</strong>, and takes its two actions on the haunt's initiative. Void damage from the haunt doesn't heal a shadow minion.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(expert)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Shadows Rise","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> More than three living creatures end their turn in area <strong>A19</strong> or any one creature ends their turn while standing within the empty bath</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Shadows seep out from the bath and swiftly coat the walls and ceiling, covering all entrances into <strong>A19</strong> with sheets of shadow, plunging the area within into darkness. Non-magical light sources, such as a torch or lantern, do not emanate any light in the room while the haunt is active. This also suppresses magical light of rank 3 or lower. The haunt then rolls initiative.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/adventure-toolbox/carrion-angel":{"lang":["Common","Diabolic","Draconic","Empyrean","Necril"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing","saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. vitality"},"flavor":"<p>Obscure and profane rites enable a blasphemer to transform a captured angel into an undead monstrosity known as a carrion angel. With their decaying flesh, foul stench of decay, and broken wings crawling with flies, none would mistake a carrion angel for anything but the atrocity they are—that is, unless the carrion angel chooses to cloak themself in a more pleasant, illusory deception. A carrion angel is not controlled by their creator, yet they generally regard their creator as an ally. Most outlast their creators, at which point they seek their own evil agendas in the world.</p>\n<p>Similar undead atrocities created via sacrifices of other types of celestials exist, and have similar abilities to the carrion angel. Yet for whatever reason, carrion angels are the most well-known and commonly encountered of these rare horrors.</p><h2>Blasphemous Choices</h2><p>The typical carrion angel presented here represents the least powerful of their kind, an undead abomination created through vile rites involving an angel's sacrifice to an evil deity. Yet sometimes, fallen angels seek undeath deliberately—not to avoid immortality, as do so many mortals, but simply to embrace an unwholesome and blasphemous new source of power. These carrion angels are often much more powerful than those who had no choice in their fate.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Vitality","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Blasphemous Stench","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","divine","olfactory"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. A creature entering or starting its turn in the aura must succeed at a DC 27 Fortitude save or become Sickened 1 (plus Slowed 1 as long as it's sickened on a critical failure). A holy creature takes a –2 status penalty to this saving throw. A creature that succeeds at its save or recovers from being sickened is temporarily immune to blasphemous stench for 1 minute unless it is holy.</p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Decaying Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["curse","divine","unholy","void"],"f":"","d":"<p>The first time each round that a living creature takes damage from a carrion angel's claw Strike, it must attempt a DC 30 Fortitude save to avoid becoming Sickened 1 and Drained 1 on a failure (Sickened 2 and Drained 2 on a critical failure).</p>"},{"n":"Living Facade","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","divine","illusion"],"f":"","d":"<p>The carrion angel hides their true appearance behind a potent illusion that makes them appear as they did in life. The carrion angel has an automatic result of 42 on Deception checks and DCs to pass as living. While Living Facade is in effect, the carrion angel loses void healing and can be healed as if they were alive. This illusion lasts until the start of the carrion angel's next turn, but the angel can Sustain it indefinitely—when the carrion angel chooses to stop Sustaining it, they cannot use Living Facade again for 24 hours. The carrion angel's blasphemous stench aura is suppressed for as long as they maintain their Living Facade.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-9-the-towers/caydserris-arudora":{"lang":["Aklo","Chthonian","Common","Elven","Necril","Sakvroth"],"gear":["Mace","Chain Mail"],"focus":3,"abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Charg's Final Gift","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>As soon as Caydserris is reduced to 0 Hit Points, he shrieks in exaltation. His head rips free from his body, which crumbles to the ground as a matrix of flesh and bone extend outward around his head, transforming him into a monstrous unique sahkil. His head and face remain, but rather than atop a humanoid body, it sits at the center of a great wheel of bone and gristle, held in place at the center by thick strings of viscera, entrails, veins, and vertebrae. Eyes and mouths open and weep along the wheel's meat-and-bristle-encrusted rim, as do five long distorted skinless arms that end in seven-fingered talons. Caydserris's wheel body floats in the air and slowly rotates, yet his head remains at all times aligned upright as the grisly spokes that connect it to the wheel constantly rip free and reattach to hold the head in place. All PCs within 60 feet must succeed at a DC 33 Will save or become Frightened 1 (Frightened 3 on a critical failure), and Caydserris regains 100 healing HP. At the same moment, Florin's soul wrenches completely free from Caydserris's dropped mace and rises up with a howl of rage to swoop back and forth through his new body.</p>\n<p>In this new form, Caydserris's Cursebound condition increases to 2 (or increases his current cursebound condition value by 1). He loses his mace Strike but gains his fearful claw Strike. He can't cast spells in this form and loses his base Speed (but not his fly Speed). If he survives, whether or not Charg allows him to eventually return to his human form is up to you.</p>"},{"n":"Florin's Revenge","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>On the start of each of Caydserris's turns, after he's gained Charg's final gift, Florin's vengeful soul deals 6d6 spirit damage to him (DC 35 basic Will save).</p>"},{"n":"Charg's Blessing","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["curse","cursebound","divine","morph"],"f":"","d":"<p>Caydserris increases his Cursebound condition's value by 1, then targets a creature he can see within 60 feet. That creature's body distorts and partially transforms, changing limbs into monstrous appendages that are difficult to control. The target must attempt a DC 36 Fortitude save with the following results; regardless of the save, the target is temporarily immune to Charg's Blessing for 24 hours.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target recovers their body's shape immediately and is unaffected; Caydserris's cursebound condition value increases by 2 instead of 1 for using this ability.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The target becomes Clumsy 1 for 1 round.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The target becomes clumsy 1 with an unlimited duration.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The target becomes Clumsy 2 with an unlimited duration.</p>"},{"n":"Curse of Ancestral Meddling","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When Caydserris has the Cursebound condition, he becomes Clumsy with a value equal to his cursebound value as Arudora spirits—particularly that of Florin Kindler—vie for control of his mind and hinder his movements.</p>"},{"n":"Terror","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>Caydserris can only make fearful claw Strikes once he receives Charg's Final Gift. A creature that takes damage from a fearful claw strike becomes Frightened 1 (Frightened 2 on a critical hit).</p>"},{"n":"Unholy Bloodshed","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate"],"f":"once per day","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per day</p>\n<p><strong>Trigger</strong> Caydserris critically succeeds at an attack roll against a holy creature with his Unholy Mace</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The target takes 3d8 bleed.</p>"},{"n":"Whispers of Weakness","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["cursebound"],"f":"","d":"<p>Spirits guide Caydserris. He increases his Cursebound value by 1, then targets one creature within 60 feet and gains knowledge of their weaknesses, which of their saving throw modifiers is lowest, and a +2 status bonus to his next attack roll against that foe before the end of his turn; the target is then temporarily immune for 1 day.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-5-the-dungeon/collapsing-lift":{"description":"<p>The anchor points in the platform's corners suddenly tear loose, causing the entire platform to collapse into the shaft below.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 32 Crafting (expert) or DC 35 Thievery (expert) to reinforce one of the anchor points; at least three of the four dangerous anchor points must be disabled to make the lift safe</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(master)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Collapsing Platform","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The lift is raised or lowered while at least one Small or larger creature stands on it</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Attempt a DC 11 Flat. This DC increases by 1 for each creature on the lift that's Medium, or by 4 for each Large creature. On a success, the lift operates normally that round, but on a failure, the anchor points give way, dropping the platform and all creatures on the lift into the shaft. A falling creature can arrest their fall by Grabbing an Edge to grab one of the swinging ropes (DC 33 Reflex), otherwise the creature takes normal falling damage plus an additional 4d6 bludgeoning damage (DC 33 basic Reflex) from falling debris.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-1-the-haunted-shore/cursed-mural":{"description":"<p>The faint image of Shelyn performing for an enraptured audience distorts into a nightmare scene of a dead god tormenting the souls of her followers.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 23 Religion (trained) to offer prayers to counteract the blasphemies or DC 20 Performance (expert) to give the tormented souls a more positive performance to enjoy; two successes are required to disable it.</p>","reset":"<p>The haunt deactivates at the end of the third consecutive round in which there are no living, sapient creatures in area <strong>A22</strong>, then resets automatically the next round.</p>","routine":"<p>(3 actions) The sounds of the undead Shelyn's performance grow audible, bringing a somber, menacing dirge to the shrine. As undead Shelyn plays, creatures depicted in the mural die horrific deaths and send blasts of tormented spiritual energy spiraling out into the shrine. The haunt uses its three actions to make lifedrain Strikes at three different sapient targets in area <strong>A22</strong>, sending a tormented, phantasmal, and gory remnant spiraling out from the mural each time. A creature can only be targeted by a lifedrain Strike once per round.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(expert)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Listen to Your Own Decay","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental","occult","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> a PC approaches within 5 feet of the mural, or a PC Seeks the mural from anywhere in area <strong>A22</strong></p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The imagery on the mural distorts. Shelyn decays into an undead blasphemy, animating into unlife as she performs for an audience suddenly thrown into torment. One by one, the animals, fey, and humanoids in the mural die grisly deaths as the dirge slaughters them. Each PC in area <strong>A22</strong> must succeed at a DC 20 Will save to avoid becoming Frightened 1 (Frightened 2 on a critical failure). The haunt then rolls initiative.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-4-the-reception-halls/danrasteian-the-magnificent":{"lang":["Alghollthu","Common","Thalassic","Truespeech"],"gear":["Longsword","Studded Leather Armor"],"notes":{"saves":"+2 status to all saves vs. auditory and visual"},"focus":1,"abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"+2 Status to All Saves vs. Auditory and Visual","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Assume Form","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","occult","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ugothol spends 10 minutes reshaping its appearance to take on the shape of any Small or Medium humanoid. It gains a +4 circumstance bonus to Deception checks to pass as that creature.</p>"},{"n":"Blood Nourishment","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The ugothol uses its three-pronged tongue to drink the blood of an adjacent Restrained or Unconscious creature. The creature gains Drained 1</p>"},{"n":"Compression","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When the ugothol successfully Squeezes, it moves through the tight space at full speed. Narrow confines are not difficult terrain for an ugothol.</p>"},{"n":"Revert Form","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The ugothol is in an assumed form</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The ugothol resumes its true form. Until the start of its next turn, it gains a +2 status bonus to attack rolls, damage rolls, saving throws, and skill checks.</p>\n<p>Effect: Revert Form</p>"},{"n":"Sneak Attack","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The ugothol deals 1d6 extra precision damage to Off-Guard creatures.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-5-the-dungeon/dazerien-arudora":{"lang":["Common","wolf empathy"],"lore":[["Bastardhall Lore",20]],"gear":["Composite Longbow","Dazerien's Thumbscrew","Bronze Key","Fearflower Nectar (10)","Arrows (20)"],"abilities":[{"n":"Wolf Empathy","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The werecreature can ask questions of, receive answers from, and use the Diplomacy skill with animals of its general kind.</p>"},{"n":"Fearful of the Frost","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Dazerien lost a few toes to frostbite, but what clings in his mind is the agony of the cold that seeped into his bones as he endured a particularly cruel punishment from his father. The first time each round he takes cold damage, Dazerien takes 2d6 mental damage and becomes Frightened 1 (Frightened 2 if the triggering cold damage was in excess of 20).</p>"},{"n":"Abduct","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["move"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> Dazerien has a Medium or smaller creature Grabbed</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Dazerien attempts to carry off his victim. The grabbed creature attempts a DC 30 Fortitude save with the following effects.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The grabbed creature escapes and is no longer grabbed.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The grabbed creature remains grabbed.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> As success, but Dazerien can Stride, and carries the grabbed creature with him. When he's Large as a result of Moon Frenzy, Dazerien can carry up to 22 Bulk without becoming encumbered—generally more than enough to haul away a Medium (6 Bulk) creature with all their gear and armor at full speed.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, plus Dazerien gains a +4 circumstance bonus to Athletics on his next attempt to Grapple the grabbed creature.</p>"},{"n":"Change Shape","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","polymorph","primal"],"f":"","d":"<ul><li><p><strong>Human</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Melee</strong> fist +24 <strong>Damage</strong> 1d4+13</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Wolf</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Speed</strong> 40 feet</p></li><li><p><strong>Knockdown</strong></p></li></ul></li></ul><hr><p>The werecreature changes into its humanoid, hybrid, or animal shape. Each shape has a specific, persistent appearance. A true werecreature's natural form is its hybrid shape.</p>\n<p>In humanoid shape, the werecreature uses its original humanoid size, loses its jaws and claws Strikes, and gains a melee fist Strike that deals bludgeoning damage equal to the slashing damage dealt by its claw.</p>\n<p>In animal shape, its Speed and size change to that of the animal, it gains any special Strike effects of the animal that it didn't already have (such as Grab), and it loses its weapon Strikes.</p><hr><p><p>The monster changes its shape indefinitely. It can use this action again to return to its natural shape or adopt a new shape. Unless otherwise noted, a monster cannot use Change Shape to appear as a specific individual. Using Change Shape counts as creating a disguise for the Impersonate use of Deception. The monster's transformation automatically defeats Perception DCs to determine whether the creature is a member of the ancestry or creature type into which it transformed, and it gains a +4 status bonus to its Deception DC to prevent others from seeing through its disguise. Change Shape abilities specify what shapes the monster can adopt. The monster doesn't gain any special abilities of the new shape, only its physical form. For example, in each shape, it replaces its normal Speeds and Strikes, and might potentially change its senses or size. Any changes are listed in its stat block.</p></p>"},{"n":"Coat Longbow","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> At least one magic oil or poison is stored inside the rune's extradimensional space</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> For 1 minute, you can apply stored oils and poisons to the weapon without needing any hands free. Applying them takes the same number of actions as normal. An oil or poison applied this way pours directly from the extradimensional space onto the weapon, and when it's fully applied, its empty vial is ejected.</p>"},{"n":"Curse of the Werewolf","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["curse","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>This curse affects only humanoids.</p><hr><p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 30 Fortitude</p>\n<p>On each full moon, the cursed creature must succeed at another Fortitude save or turn into the same kind of werecreature until dawn.</p>\n<p>The creature is under the GM's control and goes on a rampage for half the night before falling unconscious until dawn.</p>"},{"n":"Moon Frenzy","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["polymorph","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>When a full moon appears in the night sky, the werecreature must enter hybrid form, can't Change Shape thereafter, becomes one size larger, increases its reach by 5 feet, and increases the damage of its jaws by 2.</p>\n<p>When the moon sets or the sun rises, the werecreature returns to humanoid form and is Fatigued for 2d4 hours.</p>\n<p>Effect: Moon Frenzy</p>"},{"n":"Painful Wound","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> Dazerien deals damage with a melee Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Dazerien tears at the victim's flesh, dealing 1d6 bleed and causing the victim to become Sickened 1 from the pain (Sickened 2 on a failed DC 30 Fortitude save).</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Knockdown (Wolf Form)","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Knockdown in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Trip the creature. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-1-the-haunted-shore/despair-is-thy-reward":{"description":"<p>The ghosts of weeping, cowardly soldiers and the sound of claws scraping on the outer walls manifest.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 26 Intimidation (trained) to stand firm against the coming terrors and show no fear or DC 23 Religion (expert) to exorcise the haunt; two total successes are required to disable the haunt</p>","reset":"<p>The haunt deactivates and then resets after 1 round in which no living creatures are within the watch post.</p>","routine":"<p>(1 action) The sound of talons scratching on the outer walls of the room and glimpses of lean, shadowy shapes flapping and scrambling at the arrow slits manifest. A PC who takes an action to Seek these shapes and succeeds at a DC 21 Occultism check to Recall knowledge recognizes the shapes as nightgaunts. Each round on its turn, these shadowy nightgaunts reach impossibly long, thin, spade-tipped tails into the room through the arrow slits or down the stove's chimney to make a shadowy tail Strike against every creature in the room as 1 action. If the door to this area remains open, the tails have an easier time reaching inside and gain a +2 circumstance bonus on their attack rolls.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(expert)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"They're All Around Us!","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A living creature ends their turn inside the watch post</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Ghostly human soldiers manifest in the central alcove, crying out in terror as they drop their weapons and beg for mercy. Each PC in area <strong>A3</strong> becomes overwhelmed with the conviction that their own battle prowess will not save them from the danger rushing toward the building and must attempt a DC 22 Will save to avoid becoming Frightened 2 (Frightened 3 on a critical failure). The haunt then rolls initiative.</p>"},{"n":"Tickle","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature that takes damage from a shadowy tail is uncomfortably tickled by the slithering appendage and must attempt a DC 21 Fortitude save to avoid becoming Sickened 1 (Sickened 2 on a critical failure).</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-4-the-reception-halls/disgruntled-diners":{"description":"<p>Phantasmal diners begin hurling phantom food and very real, sharp cutlery in a dangerous food fight that targets intruders.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 25 Intimidation (trained) to threaten the ghostly guests into submission or DC 28 Religion (expert) to exorcise the haunt; two successes are required to disable it.</p>","reset":"<p>The haunt deactivates at the start of any round in which Ilza is the only other creature present, then immediately resets.</p>","routine":"<p>(1 action) On the haunt's initiative, the phantoms hurl ghostly food and real silverware through the room. All creatures other than Ilza who are in the area take 2d8+9 piercing damage (DC 24 basic Reflex save) from the sharp forks and knives being thrown about.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(expert)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Any Excuse for a Food Fight","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> Ilza attacks the PCs</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Upon seeing Ilza's anger, the guests rise up in a flurry of activity, their voices suddenly loud and startling. Every PC in area <strong>D6</strong> must succeed at a DC 24 Will save or become Off-Guard for the first round of combat. The haunt then rolls initiative.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-8-the-vault/eragayl-arudora":{"lang":["Common","Draconic"],"notes":{"hp":"rejuvenation, void healing"},"focus":1,"abilities":[{"n":"Site Bound","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A typical ghost can stray only a short distance from where it was killed or the place it haunts. A typical limit is 120 feet. Some ghosts are instead bound to a room, building, item, or creature that was special to it rather than a location.</p>"},{"n":"Rejuvenation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>If he's destroyed, he rejuvenates in 2d4 days unless the Bastardhall Curse is lifted.</p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Blasphemous Decree","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["divine","mental"],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Eragayl points out a creature he can see within 60 feet with his blade, proclaiming them to be heretics deserving of death. The target creature's faith quakes and strains under the decree, and the target creature must attempt a DC 29 Will save with the following effects. Worshippers of Shelyn gain a +2 status bonus to this saving throw. This ability replaces the ghost mage's arcane innate spells.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected. If the creature is a worshipper of Shelyn, Eragayl is Stunned 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes 2d8 mental damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes 4d8 mental damage. If the creature can cast divine spells, it's Stupefied 1 for 1 round.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes 8d8 mental damage. If the creature can cast divine spells, it is stupefied 1 for 1 round and can't cast divine spells for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Frightful Moan","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","divine","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ghost laments its fate, forcing each living creature within 30 feet to attempt a DC 29 Will save. On a failure, a creature becomes Frightened 2 (or Frightened 3 on a critical failure). On a success, a creature is temporarily immune to this ghost's frightful moan for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Reminders of Life","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Eragayl is shocked if anyone addresses him by the nickname his wife Tasifinie had for him: \"tiger.\" The first time someone does so in a round, Eragayl must attempt a DC 35 Will save to avoid becoming Slowed 1 for 1 round by the distraction. If the person who used this name on him openly wields Fearcutter, his memories usurp his nature automatically; he shrieks in rage and turns his wrath on Irrokcis instead, cursing his son's name as he uses a combination of Blasphemous Decrees and Telekinetic Assault on the lich for the rest of the combat.</p>"},{"n":"Telekinetic Assault","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ghost cries out in pain and anguish as small objects and debris fly about in a 30-foot emanation. Creatures in this area take 6d6 bludgeoning damage, subject to a DC 29 basic Reflex save.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/adventure-toolbox/esmira-vermidian":{"lang":["Common"],"notes":{"saves":"–1 status penalty to all saves vs. death effects"},"abilities":[{"n":"Bound to Bastardhall","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Unlike the typical phantom, Esmira exists fully in the Ethereal Plane and cannot shift back and forth between this plane and the Universe.</p>"},{"n":"–1 Status Penalty to All Saves vs. Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Bound Even in Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>As phantom, but if Esmira is reduced to 0 Hit Points by any means, her soul does not return to the River of Souls. Instead, her soul is drawn into the soul vortex in the vault under Bastardhall (area <strong>I</strong>).</p>"},{"n":"Physical Manifestation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Upon reaching rank 1, Esmira manifests a semi-physical body. She loses the incorporeal trait, her immunities and resistances, and her fly Speed, and gains a land Speed of 25 feet. She has a Strength modifier of +1, an Athletics modifier of +8, an AC of 21, 72 HP, and arcane innate spells. Her other statistics do not change.</p>"},{"n":"Phantom Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["spirit"],"f":"","d":"<p>Each time they make a Strike, a phantom can choose to deal spirit damage instead of the normal physical damage type.</p>"},{"n":"Spellcasting","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Once Esmira is rank 1 and has a physical body, she gains the following arcane innate spells. As her rank increases, her arcane spell DC and spell attack modifier increase, and she gains additional innate spells.</p>\n<p><strong>Arcane Innate Spells</strong> DC 22, attack +14; <strong>3rd</strong> Fireball, Haste; 2nd Dispel Magic, Invisibility, Resist Energy; 1st Charm, Force Barrage, Illusory Object; <strong>Cantrips (3rd)</strong> Detect Magic, Electric Arc, Light, Read Aura, Telekinetic Hand</p>\n<p><strong>Rank 2</strong> DC 24, attack +16; <strong>4th</strong> Fly; <strong>3rd</strong> Slow. Her cantrips are heightened to 4th rank.</p>\n<p><strong>Rank 3</strong> DC 26, attack +18; <strong>5th</strong> Impaling Spike; <strong>4th</strong> Translocate. Her cantrips are heightened to 5th rank.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-4-the-reception-halls/ghostlight-sister":{"lang":["Common","Necril","Varisian"],"lore":[["Theater Lore",13]],"gear":["Maestro's Intrument"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"abilities":[{"n":"Rhapsodic Flourish","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["occult","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The Ghostlight Sister would roll a save against an auditory or sonic effect</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The Ghostlight Sister performs a refrain that deflects sonic vibrations and rolls a Performance check in place of the triggering saving throw. Sonic feedback rebounds from the effect, dealing 1d6 sonic damage to the source of the triggering effect if the Performance check succeeded or 2d6 sonic damage to the Ghostlight Sister on a failure.</p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Soloist's Curse","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["curse","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> Trio's Torment is in effect</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The Ghostlight Sister performs a haunting solo. This sustains an ongoing Trio's Torment if at least one other Ghostlight Sister is taking part. The Ghostlight Sister targets a single creature she can see within 60 feet as the focus of her unsettling music; that creature must attempt a DC 24 Will save with the following effects. The Ghostlight Sister can't use Soloist's Curse again for 1 minute; if all Ghostlight Sisters in a Trio's Torment can't use Soloist's Curse, then the ongoing Trio's Torment ends and all three Ghostlight Sisters can't use Trio's Torment for 1 minute.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes 3d6 mental damage and becomes Clumsy 1 for 1 round as the music dredges up what feel like repressed memories of forgotten agonies.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> As success, but 6d6 mental damage and clumsy 1 for 1 hour.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but 12d6 mental damage and clumsy 1 with an unlimited duration.</p>"},{"n":"Trio's Torment","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The Ghostlight Sister begins playing a catchy song that compels others to dance. If other Ghostlight Sisters within 30 feet of her want to join the Trio's Torment, the Ghostlight Sister can delay to begin the torment. In this case, the effect occurs after the last Ghostlight Sister uses Trio's Torment or when one of them chooses to complete that round of the torment. Each non–Ghostlight Sister in a 50-foot emanation must attempt a DC 24 Will save; for each additional Ghostlight Sister involved, the save DC increases by 1 (to a maximum of DC 26). The effect lasts until the end of the next round, but if all of the Ghostlight Sisters choose to Sustain the effect on all affected creatures that remain within the emanation, the effect persists for one additional round.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected and is temporarily immune for 1 hour.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature can't use actions with the move trait except to dance, which uses the Stride action to move up to half the creature's Speed. If the creature has other Speeds, it can also dance using those modes of travel. If it's after dark, the creature becomes Frightened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> As success, and the creature must spend at least 1 action each turn dancing; the creature becomes Frightened 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the creature becomes Frightened 3.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-4-the-reception-halls/ghostly-guest":{"lang":["Common","Varisian"],"notes":{"hp":"rejuvenation, void healing"},"abilities":[{"n":"Site Bound","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The ghostly guests cannot leave the reception halls.</p>"},{"n":"Rejuvenation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>When a ghostly guest is destroyed, they reform after 2d4 days fully healed and can be encountered again. They're only permanently destroyed once the Bastardhall Curse is lifted at the end of the Adventure Path.</p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Join Us!","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","emotion","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ghostly guest Flies over to a space adjacent to a creature, then extends their arms in an invitation to join the ghost in whatever form of revelry it appears to be engaged in at the moment, be it dancing, drinking, or eating. The target must attempt a DC 24 Will save to resist the invitation. On a failed save, roll 1d6 to determine what the ghost's current revelry is and what effect the creature endures. If the target fails its save against Join Us! while already enduring a condition inflicted by a previous failed save, the new condition replaces the old condition, and the change in condition leaves the character Off-Guard for 1 round from the disorienting sensations.</p>\n<p><strong>1–2 Dancing</strong> The creature suddenly feels as if they've been dancing the night away. Aches and pains settle into their bodies, and they become Enfeebled 1 (Enfeebled 2 on a critical failure) for 1 hour.</p>\n<p><strong>3–4 Drinking</strong> The creature suddenly feels dizzy and drunk and becomes Stupefied 1 (Stupefied 2 on a critical failure) for 1 hour.</p>\n<p><strong>5–6 Eating</strong> The creature suddenly feels bloated and overfull and becomes Clumsy 1 (Clumsy 2 on a critical failure) for 1 hour.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/ghoulish-gardener":{"lang":["Common","Necril"],"lore":[["Carpentry Lore",12]],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"abilities":[{"n":"Stench","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","olfactory"],"f":"","d":"<p>10 feet. DC 19 Fortitude</p><hr><p><p>A creature entering the aura or starting its turn in the area must succeed at a Fortitude save or become Sickened 1 (plus Slowed 1 as long as it's sickened on a critical failure). A creature that succeeds at its save or recovers from being sickened is temporarily immune to all stench auras for 1 minute.</p></p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Consume Flesh","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The ghoul is adjacent to the corpse of a creature that died within the last hour.</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul devours a chunk of the corpse and regains 3d6 healing Hit Points.</p>\n<p>It can regain Hit Points from any given corpse only once.</p>"},{"n":"Ghoul Whispers","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","linguistic","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> A Grabbed, Paralyzed, Restrained, or Unconscious creature is within the ghoul's reach</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul whispers dark thoughts and vile cravings into the creature's ears. The creature must save against the forbidden cravings curse.</p>\n<p><strong>Forbidden Cravings</strong> (curse) A creature can still eat and drink while sickened by this curse</p>\n<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 22 Will</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 1</strong> carrier with no ill effects (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 2d6 void damage and the target is Sickened 1 until it consumes raw meat (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 3</strong> as stage 2</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 4</strong> as stage 2 unless the target has consumed raw meat in the past 24 hours, then it takes 4d6 void damage and is Sickened 2 until it consumes raw meat;</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 5</strong> if the creature has eaten raw meat in the past 24 hours, it dies and rises as a ghoul, if not, it returns to stage 4</p>"},{"n":"Grave Knowledge","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["occult"],"f":"once per hour","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per hour</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul calls upon knowledge it retains from one creature it has consumed in the past 7 days. The ghoul attempts a skill check using a skill in which the consumed creature was trained (if it's unclear whether the creature was trained, the GM decides). The ghoul is treated as trained and has a +13 skill modifier. This takes the same amount of actions or time as usual for the check.</p>\n<p>The ghoul can instead automatically learn something specific known by a creature it consumed in the last 7 days, like the location of a Hidden treasure or the name of a loved one. The ghoul can do this only once for a given creature, no matter how much of its flesh the ghoul consumed.</p>"},{"n":"Swift Leap","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["move"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ghoul jumps up to half its Speed. This movement doesn't trigger reactions.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/ghoulrilla":{"lang":["Common","Necril"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"abilities":[{"n":"Stench","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","olfactory"],"f":"","d":"<p>10 feet. DC 23 Fortitude</p><hr><p><p>A creature entering the aura or starting its turn in the area must succeed at a Fortitude save or become Sickened 1 (plus Slowed 1 as long as it's sickened on a critical failure). A creature that succeeds at its save or recovers from being sickened is temporarily immune to all stench auras for 1 minute.</p></p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Consume Flesh","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The ghoul is adjacent to the corpse of a creature that died within the last hour.</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul devours a chunk of the corpse and regains 5d6 healing Hit Points.</p>\n<p>It can regain Hit Points from any given corpse only once.</p>"},{"n":"Ghoul Whispers","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","linguistic","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> A Grabbed, Paralyzed, Restrained, or Unconscious creature is within the ghoul's reach</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul whispers dark thoughts and vile cravings into the creature's ears. The creature must save against the forbidden cravings curse.</p>\n<p><strong>Forbidden Cravings</strong> (curse) A creature can still eat and drink while sickened by this curse</p>\n<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 26 Will</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 1</strong> carrier with no ill effects (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 2d6 void damage and the target is Sickened 1 until it consumes raw meat (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 3</strong> as stage 2</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 4</strong> as stage 2 unless the target has consumed raw meat in the past 24 hours, then it takes 4d6 void damage and is Sickened 2 until it consumes raw meat;</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 5</strong> if the creature has eaten raw meat in the past 24 hours, it dies and rises as a ghoul, if not, it returns to stage 4</p>"},{"n":"Grisly Display","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ghoulrilla howls and claws at its flesh, flinging bits of matted fur, gristle, and rancid meat in a nauseating display. The ghoulrilla takes 1d6 slashing damage, and all creatures within 30-foot emanation feet must attempt a DC 23 Fortitude save. While a creature is sickened by this ability, it is Off-Guard to the ghoulrilla.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creatures is unaffected and temporarily immune for 1 minute.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Sickened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Sickened 2, and a piece of the ghoulrilla's body or rancid blood got into the creature's mouth, open wound, or otherwise infected them—the creature is exposed to forbidden cravings (see Ghoul Whispers).</p>"},{"n":"Swift Leap","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["move"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ghoul jumps up to half its Speed. This movement doesn't trigger reactions.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-8-the-vault/gogiteth-bastion-of-blasphemies":{"lang":["Sakvroth","(Can't Speak)"],"notes":{"ac":"all-around vision"},"flavor":"<p>A gogiteth is a slavering nightmare of teeth, eyes, and hairy spiderlike legs, and its appearance is invariably seared into the minds of any who witness it. Hives of these skittering monsters haunt the lowest reaches of the Darklands, competing with cave worms and other subterranean horrors for food and resources. A gogiteth is rarely alone, as they learned that the best means of survival is sticking with others of their own kind. Even the hardiest Darklands residents seek cover when a gogiteth is spotted, for where there's one, a swarm is sure to follow.</p>\n<p>Gogiteths make a clacking sound as they skitter. The joints in their many legs pop and crack with each movement, though they can suppress this voluntarily and hunt silently without alerting prey. The odd creatures can also make a high-pitched whistling sound that echoes throughout the caverns where they live. Some Darklands natives report that groups of gogiteths sometimes join together in eerie, discordant songs.</p>\n<p>Gogiteth anatomy have inspired no shortage of speculation as to their origins. Some believe they are the result of a fleshwarping experiment gone horribly wrong. Others think they may be related in some way to the Dominion of the Black—possibly the distant cousins or some strange exiles of those alien entities. According to this mythos, once they made their way to Golarion, the gogiteths crawled into the depths of the earth, shying away from the blazing sun above.</p>\n<p>Gogiteths are a menace to every other denizen of the Darklands. Those who live in gogiteth-infested regions of Sekamina or Orv frequently call for temporary truces when a gogiteth swarm has been spotted. Since even an average gogiteth hive can host up to two dozen of the horrors, calls to eradicate them are dangerous quests indeed.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"All-Around Vision","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>This monster can see in all directions simultaneously and therefore can't be flanked.</p></p>"},{"n":"Skittering Reposition","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["move"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature that starts its move outside the gogiteth's reach moves into its reach.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The gogiteth moves 10 feet. This does not trigger reactions.</p>"},{"n":"Carry Off Prey","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The gogiteth can move at its full Speed while it has a creature Grabbed in its jaws, bringing the grabbed creature along.</p>"},{"n":"Constrict","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>3d6+12 bludgeoning, DC 32 basic Fortitude</p>\n<hr>\n<p><p>The monster deals the listed amount of damage to any number of creatures Grabbed or Restrained by it. Each of those creatures can attempt a basic Fortitude save with the listed DC.</p></p>"},{"n":"Skittering Assault","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The gogiteth Strides three times. Once per Stride, it can attempt a leg Strike against a creature in its reach at any point during the Stride; it must make each attack against a different creature, but it doesn't apply its multiple attack penalty until after making all its Strikes. If any of the Strikes result in a critical failure, Skittering Assault ends.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Grab","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Improved Grab as an action and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Improved Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-4-the-reception-halls/grampa-sizzle":{"lang":["Common","Varisian"],"abilities":[{"n":"Personality Fragments","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A soulbound doll shares fragments of its donor soul's personality, though none of that creature's memories. This causes a soulbound doll to match a strong personality trait of the donor soul. Because of its soul sliver, a soulbound doll is not immune to spirit as most constructs are.</p>\n<p>When Grampa Sizzle casts Vision of Death, targets experience the terror of burning alive, and when he casts Phantom Pain, the targets suddenly recall painful burns.</p>"},{"n":"Pratfall","a":"r","c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> Grampa Sizzle fails or critically fails an attack roll or a skill check</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Grampa Sizzle makes a quick scene out of his failure, such as by falling and suddenly bouncing back to his feet or making a ridiculous face. If he has a Laughing Fit spell available, he can cast it as part of this reaction.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/grinder-widget":{"abilities":[{"n":"Tremorsense 30 feet","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>Tremorsense allows a monster to feel the vibrations through a solid surface caused by movement. It is an imprecise sense with a limited range (listed in the ability). Tremorsense functions only if the monster is on the same surface as the subject, and only if the subject is moving along (or burrowing through) the surface.</p></p>"},{"n":"Infiltration Tools","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A dig-widget's face consists of a set of Infiltrator Thieves' Tools. They can be salvaged from a destroyed dig-widget with a successful DC 20 Crafting check. On a failed check, the tools are destroyed.</p>"},{"n":"Mechanical Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature with expert proficiency in Thievery can attempt a check to Disable device to damage a dig-widget. The DC is 20, and each success deals 18 untyped damage.</p>"},{"n":"Fastening Leap","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The dig-widget Leaps up 20 feet onto a creature or object and attempts a corkscrew Strike against it. If the Strike damages the target, the dig-widget attaches to the target (typically to the back of a creature). This is similar to Grabbing the creature, but the dig-widget moves with that creature rather than holding it in place. While attached, the dig-widget can't use its corkscrew. The dig-widget can be Shoved off, or it can detach itself with an Interact action.</p>"},{"n":"Grind Bones","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate","move"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> A grinder-widget adjacent to the millstone in area <strong>C41</strong> has a creature Grabbed</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The grinder-widget attaches pliers to one of the millstone's handles and Strides around it, causing the Grabbed character to be painfully ground and pulped by the stone. The ground character must attempt a DC 23 Reflex save with the following results.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The character is unaffected and automatically Escapes from the grapple.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The character takes 1d8+2 bludgeoning damage and then automatically Escapes from the grapple.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The character takes 2d8+2 bludgeoning damage and becomes Clumsy 1 for 1 hour from wrenched and sprained limbs; the character remains grappled by the grinder-widget.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but 4d8+2 bludgeoning damage and clumsy 1 for 24 hours.</p>"},{"n":"Sneak Attack","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A dig-widget's Strikes deal an additional 1d6 precision damage to Off-Guard creatures.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-1-the-haunted-shore/grindylow-stalker":{"lang":["Thalassic"],"gear":["Bola (3)"],"flavor":"<p>The top half of a grindylow vaguely resembles a goblin, but from the waist down, their bodies split into a tangle of suckered, wriggling tentacles. Grindylows mostly dwell in shallow waters, fresh and briny, including lakes, rivers, coastal regions, and coral reefs. They generally organize into groups called schools, which can range from a few individuals to a few hundred. Smaller schools are sometimes taken over by a powerful aquatic creature, though such alliances only last until the school faces a major setback, at which point the surviving grindylows scatter and form smaller schools of their own.</p>\n<p>Grindylows aren't territorial, but they are pragmatic. While they rarely build permanent structures, they will adopt a good hunting ground for generations until driven away by predators. They often lair in mobile shelters, such as a sargasso of seaweed or hull of an abandoned ship. They are skilled scavengers and hunters who eat anything they can sink their teeth into.</p>\n<p>Grindylows respect the power of larger sea predators but have a special hatred for squids (or anything that resembles a squid). Sailors plying grindylow-infested waters often paint the images of squids on the bottoms of their hulls in hopes of warding off potentials raids. While this can keep smaller schools at bay, it can also backfire, potentially inciting larger groups to gather for a coordinated attack; this becomes especially likely if the ship's route is predictable. Their hatred of squids does not extend to other tentacled creatures; grindylows consider octopuses to be the epitome of grace and power.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Reactive Strike (Special)","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A grindylow gains 1 extra reaction at the start of each of their turns that they can use only to make a Reactive Strike with a tentacle. They can't use more than one Reactive Strike triggered by the same action or choice.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Clinging Suckers","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a grindylow successfully Grabs a creature larger than themself, they attach to that creature. The Grabbed creature is not Immobilized, but if it moves, the grindylow moves with it.</p>\n<p>If the creature is Medium or smaller, it takes a –5-foot status penalty to its Speeds while the grindylow is attached. The grindylow is Off-Guard while attached to a creature.</p>\n<p>Effect: Clinging Suckers</p>"},{"n":"Jet","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["move"],"f":"","d":"<p>The grindylow moves up to 60 feet in a straight line through the water without triggering reactions.</p>"},{"n":"Sneak Attack","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A grindylow stalker deals 1d4 extra precision damage to creatures who are Off-Guard.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-2-castle-grounds/haunted-coop":{"description":"<p>A sudden flurry of shadowy pigeons erupts into a frenzy and breaks out of the coop.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 25 Nature to recognize the unquiet animal spirits and soothe them, allowing the spirits to disperse</p>","reset":"<p>The haunted coop resets 1d4 rounds after it's triggered.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(expert)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"The Pigeons! The Pigeons!","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["curse","emotion","fear","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A person attempts to open the coop, searches the mounds within, or causes damage to it</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> A sudden blast of shadowy pigeons emerges from the coop, shrieking and flapping into the face of the triggering creature anywhere they might be in the room. That creature must attempt a DC 27 Will save with the following results.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected and temporarily immune to these haunts for 24 hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature becomes Frightened 1, and this frightened condition can't be reduced below 1 as long as they remain in area <strong>B2a</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature becomes Frightened 2 and convinced that the pigeons are harbingers of a greater doom awaiting them. They cannot reduce their frightened condition below 1 for 24 hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but with an unlimited duration.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/adventure-toolbox/ilana-greenbough":{"lang":["Common"],"notes":{"saves":"–1 status penalty to all saves vs. death effects"},"abilities":[{"n":"Bound to Bastardhall","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Unlike the typical phantom, Ilana exists fully in the Ethereal Plane and cannot shift back and forth between this plane and the Universe.</p>"},{"n":"–1 Status Penalty to All Saves vs. Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Bound Even in Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>As phantom, but if Ilana is reduced to 0 Hit Points by any means, her soul does not return to the River of Souls. Instead, her soul is drawn into the soul vortex in the vault under Bastardhall (area <strong>I</strong>).</p>"},{"n":"Physical Manifestation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Upon reaching rank 1, Ilana manifests a semi-physical body. She loses the incorporeal trait, her immunities and resistances, and her fly Speed, and gains a land Speed of 25 feet. She has a Strength modifier of +3, an Athletics modifier of +10, an AC of 21, 74 HP, and occult innate spells. Her other statistics do not change.</p>"},{"n":"Phantom Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["spirit"],"f":"","d":"<p>Each time they make a Strike, a phantom can choose to deal spirit damage instead of the normal physical damage type.</p>"},{"n":"Spellcasting","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Once Ilana is rank 1 and has a physical body, she gains the following occult innate spells. As her rank increases, her occult spell DC and spell attack modifier increase, and she gains additional innate spells.</p>\n<p><strong>Occult Innate Spells</strong> DC 22, attack +14; <strong>3rd</strong> Locate, Soothe; <strong>2nd</strong> Clear Mind, Everlight, Soothe; <strong>1st</strong> Force Barrage, Mending, Soothe; <strong>Cantrips (3rd)</strong> Daze, Detect Magic, Light, Read Aura, Telekinetic Projectile</p>\n<p><strong>Rank 2</strong> DC 24, attack +16; <strong>4th</strong> Clear Mind; <strong>3rd</strong> Hypercognition. Her cantrips are heightened to 4th rank.</p>\n<p><strong>Rank 3</strong> DC 26, attack +18; <strong>5th</strong> Dreaming Potential; <strong>4th</strong> Soothe. Her cantrips are heightened to 5th rank.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-1-the-haunted-shore/ilgrunthin":{"lang":["Thalassic","Common"],"gear":["Leather Armor","Wand of Paranoia (Rank 2)","Gold Ring set with Tiny Pearls"],"flavor":"<p>The top half of a grindylow vaguely resembles a goblin, but from the waist down, their bodies split into a tangle of suckered, wriggling tentacles. Grindylows mostly dwell in shallow waters, fresh and briny, including lakes, rivers, coastal regions, and coral reefs. They generally organize into groups called schools, which can range from a few individuals to a few hundred. Smaller schools are sometimes taken over by a powerful aquatic creature, though such alliances only last until the school faces a major setback, at which point the surviving grindylows scatter and form smaller schools of their own.</p>\n<p>Grindylows aren't territorial, but they are pragmatic. While they rarely build permanent structures, they will adopt a good hunting ground for generations until driven away by predators. They often lair in mobile shelters, such as a sargasso of seaweed or hull of an abandoned ship. They are skilled scavengers and hunters who eat anything they can sink their teeth into.</p>\n<p>Grindylows respect the power of larger sea predators but have a special hatred for squids (or anything that resembles a squid). Sailors plying grindylow-infested waters often paint the images of squids on the bottoms of their hulls in hopes of warding off potentials raids. While this can keep smaller schools at bay, it can also backfire, potentially inciting larger groups to gather for a coordinated attack; this becomes especially likely if the ship's route is predictable. Their hatred of squids does not extend to other tentacled creatures; grindylows consider octopuses to be the epitome of grace and power.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Reactive Strike (Special)","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A grindylow gains 1 extra reaction at the start of each of their turns that they can use only to make a Reactive Strike with a tentacle. They can't use more than one Reactive Strike triggered by the same action or choice.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Clinging Suckers","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a grindylow successfully Grabs a creature larger than themself, they attach to that creature. The Grabbed creature is not Immobilized, but if it moves, the grindylow moves with it.</p>\n<p>If the creature is Medium or smaller, it takes a –5-foot status penalty to its Speeds while the grindylow is attached. The grindylow is Off-Guard while attached to a creature.</p>\n<p>Effect: Clinging Suckers</p>"},{"n":"Jet","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["move"],"f":"","d":"<p>The grindylow moves up to 60 feet in a straight line through the water without triggering reactions.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-8-the-vault/irrokcis-arudora":{"lang":["Aklo","Chthonian","Common","Daemonic","Empyrean","Necril","Requian","Sakvroth"],"gear":["Accursed Staff (Major)","Irrokcis's Medallion","Scroll of Vampiric Exsanguination (Rank 6) (4)","Scroll of Translocate (Rank 5) (4)","Wand of Scrying (Rank 6)","Wand of Soothe (Rank 5)","Retrieval Belt (Major)"],"notes":{"hp":"rejuvenation, void healing","saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. vitality"},"focus":3,"abilities":[{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Vitality","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Arachnophobia","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The first time in a round that a creature utilizes any spider-themed action in Irrokcis's presence (such as casting illusory creature to create a spider, or using summon animal to summon spiders), the lich becomes Slowed 1 for 1 round from his horror. As long as a spider is visible to him (including a work of art, such as the Horrid Figurine from area <strong>F14</strong>), he takes a –1 status penalty to saves against fear effects and loses steady spellcasting. The <em>horrid figurine</em> from area <strong>F14</strong> is particularly effective if used against Irrokcis—increase that item's save DCs to 32 when they're used against the lich.</p>"},{"n":"Frightful Presence","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>60 feet. DC 30 Will</p><hr><p><p>A creature that first enters the area must attempt a Will save.</p>\n<p>Regardless of the result of the saving throw, the creature is temporarily immune to this monster's Frightful Presence for 1 minute.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected by the presence.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 4.</p></p>"},{"n":"Rejuvenation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["arcane"],"f":"","d":"<p>When a lich is destroyed, its soul immediately transfers to their <em>Lich Soul Cage</em>. A lich can be permanently destroyed only if their <em>soul cage</em> is found and destroyed.</p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Drain Soul Cage","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"once per day","d":"<p>7th rank</p><hr><p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per day</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The lich taps into their <em>Soul Cage</em>'s power to cast any arcane spell up to the highest rank the lich can cast, even if the spell being cast is not one of the lich's prepared spells. The lich's soul cage doesn't need to be present for the lich to use this ability.</p>"},{"n":"Reach Spell","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","spellshape"],"f":"","d":"<p>If the next action Irrokcis takes is to Cast a Spell that has a range, increase that spell's range by 30 feet.</p>"},{"n":"Siphon Life","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A lich's form draws forth life from those who come into contact with it. When the lich damages a living creature with an unarmed attack, the lich gains 5 temporary Hit Points and the creature must succeed at a DC 35 Fortitude save or become Drained 1.</p>\n<p>If the lich is Grabbed or Restrained at the start of its turn, each creature grabbing or restraining it must succeed at a Fortitude save or become drained 1. If the lich siphons a creature's life again, the drained value increase by 1, to a maximum of Drained 4.</p>"},{"n":"Steady Spellcasting","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>As long as Irrokcis isn't in the presence of spiders, if a reaction would disrupt Irrokcis's spellcasting action, he attempts a DC 15 Flat. On a success, the action isn't disrupted.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-4-the-reception-halls/its-never-enough":{"description":"<p>The smells and sights of fresh baked goods compel those who experience them to take part in a grisly feast.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 28 Survival (trained) to draw upon memories of leaner times and counteract the temptation of the promised meal or DC 31 Religion (expert) to exorcise the haunt; 2 successes are required to disable it, and once the haunt is disabled, the food on the table is revealed to be foul, rotting, and moldy.</p>","reset":"<p>The haunt deactivates at the start of any round in which there are no living creatures in area <strong>D2</strong>, then reactivates 1 minute later.</p>","routine":"<p>(1 action; mental) On its initiative, the haunt exudes mental compulsions to all living creatures in the room, driving them to take part in the offered feast. Each living creature in the room must attempt a DC 26 Will save with the following results. Food consumed during this hazard's routine is automatically replenished at the start of the next round.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is compelled to approach the table of food and be seated. On the creature's turn, it Strides as often as needed to reach the table, then Interacts with a chair to be seated. If it has any remaining actions, it can use them normally (such as by Interacting again to stand up from being seated at the table).</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> As success, but the creature must spend at least one of its actions this turn (if any are available after potentially approaching and taking a seat) Interacting with the food to eat some of it. Eating the food afflicts the creature with Rancid Breakfast.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the creature must use any actions not used to approach the table or be seated at it to Interact and eat food.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(expert)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Break Your Fast","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A living creature enters area <strong>D2</strong></p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The sight and scent of the baked goods grows nearly irresistible. The triggering creature must succeed at a DC 26 Will save to avoid becoming Stupefied 1 (Stupefied 2 on a critical failure) for 24 hours. The hazard then rolls initiative.</p>"},{"n":"Rancid Breakfast","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 26 Fortitude (this increases to DC 28 Fortitude if a creature spent two actions eating or DC 30 Fortitude if a creature spent three actions eating)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 1</strong> Clumsy 1 (1 round) and 1d6 poison</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 2</strong> clumsy 1 (1 hour) and 2d6 poison</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 3</strong> Clumsy 2 (24 hours) and 4d6 poison</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 4</strong> clumsy 2 (unlimited duration) and 6d6 poison</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-7-the-fallen-temple/jazlin":{"lang":["Common"],"abilities":[{"n":"Site Bound","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>These three ghosts can only leave the crypts when Bastardhall appears on Golarion, and even then, they can't leave the island.</p>"},{"n":"Rejuvenation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>The three adventurers re-form after 2d4 days here in the crypts unless someone carries their respective urn of ashes down to the temple in Maiserene (area <strong>A9</strong>) to be cleansed by Shirail.</p>"},{"n":"Jazlin's Haunted Arrows","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>Jazlin manifests a shortbow and fires a ghostly, shrieking arrow at a target he can see within 30 feet. The target must attempt a DC 28 Will save with the following results.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target is unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The target takes 2d6 spirit damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The target takes 4d6 spirit damage and is Frightened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The target takes 8d6 spirit damage and is Frightened 3.</p>"},{"n":"Malevolent Possession","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["incapacitation","mental","possession"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ghost attempts to possess an adjacent corporeal creature. This has the same effect as the Possession spell (DC 23 Will), except since the ghost doesn't have a physical body, it is unaffected by that restriction of the spell.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-2-castle-grounds/jinnivere-arudora":{"lang":["Common","Necril"],"gear":["Bastard Sword","Studded Leather Armor","Jinnivere's Riding Crop"],"notes":{"saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. magic"},"abilities":[{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Magic","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Fast Healing 5","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A monster with this ability regains the given number of Hit Points each round at the beginning of its turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Easy to Enrage","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["auditory","concentrate","emotion","linguistic","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>Jinnivere still carries anger and frustration about the way her brother Augsten used to mercilessly tease her agility and lanky frame. A PC can attempt to insult her in the same way by making a special attempt to Demoralize her with a DC 24 Intimidation check, with the following results.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The insult cuts deep. Jinnivere loses the effects of her Head Hunter ability and Frightful Presence for the next hour, becomes Slowed 1 on her next turn as she spends one of her actions sputtering and seething in anger. The DC for her to Command an Animal on her mount rises to DC 28. The next attempt to enrage her using this insult by any PC gains a +4 circumstance bonus to the Intimidation check.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> Jinnivere sputters with anger at the insult and loses the effects of her Head Hunter ability and Frightful Presence until the end of her next turn. On her next turn, she focuses her attacks on the insulting PC (unless a different PC achieves this result before her turn, in which case her anger shifts to that PC). The next attempt to enrage her using this insult by any PC gains a +2 circumstance bonus to the Intimidation check.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> As success, but Jinnivere retains her Head Hunter ability and no circumstance bonus is granted.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> Jinnivere is annoyed but not otherwise bothered by the insult. The PC who critically failed the check cannot attempt to enrage her in this way again for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Frightful Presence","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. DC 23 Will</p><hr><p><p>A creature that first enters the area must attempt a Will save.</p>\n<p>Regardless of the result of the saving throw, the creature is temporarily immune to this monster's Frightful Presence for 1 minute.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected by the presence.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 4.</p></p>"},{"n":"Reactive Strike","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Agonizing Welts","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["curse","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>Jinnivere can wield her riding crop as a melee weapon to create agonizing pain in the minds of those she strikes. It also inflict a curse that causes their mental pain to manifest as painful visible welts and seeping wounds that never seem to heal. If she inflicts agonizing welts on a creature already afflicted by it, the victim attempts a new save, ignoring the result if it's better than a failure.</p><hr><p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 26 Fortitude</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 1</strong> Clumsy 1 (1 round)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 2</strong> Clumsy 2 and all speeds are reduced by 10 feet (1 round)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 3</strong> As stage 2, except Clumsy 3 (1 round)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 4</strong> As stage 2, except clumsy 3 and Slowed 1 (1 round)</p>"},{"n":"Head Hunter","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Any slashing weapon gains the <em>Keen</em> rune while a dullahan wields it, and any hatchet they wield gains the <em>Returning</em> rune as well. If the dullahan kills a creature with a critical hit using a slashing weapon, the target is decapitated as though the dullahan had used Reap on the target.</p>"},{"n":"Reap","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The dullahan removes the head of a dead creature within reach. Each creature within the area of the dullahan's frightful presence must attempt a new save, even if it is temporarily immune.</p>"},{"n":"Summon Steed","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The dullahan summons a war horse with elite adjustments and the fiend and unholy traits. This steed remains until it is slain, the dullahan Dismisses this effect, or the dullahan Summons a Steed again.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/join-us-forever":{"description":"<p>The skeletal remains in the cells clatter up to their feet and reach out toward visitors as they call out, inviting them to embrace the addiction of imprisonment.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 26 Deception (trained) to trick one of the skeletons into thinking they're free, or DC 23 Religion (expert) to exorcise a haunted skeleton; each success disables one of the four haunted skeletons.</p>","reset":"<p>The haunt deactivates at the end of any round in which there are no living creatures in the room. It then resets 1 minute later.</p>","routine":"<p>(4 actions) Each of the four skeletons targets a living creature in area <strong>C37</strong>, reaches their arms out toward the target, and in a mournful yet strangely compelling voice invites the target to join them in imprisonment forever, promising blissful enlightenment in return for an eternity spent behind bars. The skeletons attempt to target a different living creature, but if there are more skeletons than targets, they randomly target identical creatures. Targeted creatures must attempt a DC 26 Will save, with a cumulative status penalty of –1 for each additional skeleton targeting them (to a maximum penalty of –3 if all four skeletons target a single creature), and the following results. This is an incapacitation and mental effect.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is tempted to remain in the room, takes 2d6 mental damage, and becomes Slowed 1 until the end of its next turn.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is compelled to remain in the room, takes 5d6 mental damage, and becomes Paralyzed until the end of its next turn.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is compelled to remain in the room, takes 10d6 mental damage, becomes Stupefied 1 with an unlimited duration, and becomes paralyzed until the end of its next turn.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(expert)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"We've Been Here Forever","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":["divine","emotion","fear","incapacitation","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A living creature ends their turn inside of area <strong>C37</strong></p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The skeletons rise up in a horrifying clatter. All living creatures in area <strong>C37</strong> must attempt a DC 26 Will save to avoid becoming Paralyzed for 1 round (or 1 minute on a critical failure, with a new save at the end of their turn to recover). The haunt then rolls initiative.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-5-the-dungeon/junth-rundel":{"flavor":"<p>This construct is a walking alchemical nightmare capable of inflicting all manner of painful wounds. The noxious needlers' ability to follow orders is granted by the otherwise mindless humanoid brain that floats in their dome-like heads, allowing them to serve as laborers and guards for their creators.</p>\n<p>In exceptionally rare cases, the brain used in their creation might retain fragments of memories or even an actual intellect, resulting in a noxious needler with a personality and agenda of its own. Unwilling creations often hunt down their creators, venting their rage on similar targets if revenge is impossible. Others blankly replicate the experiments from their last memory</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Alchemical Chambers","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A noxious needler's body contains six alchemical chambers filled with different substances. When a noxious needler's ability calls upon a randomly determined alchemical effect, roll 1d6 and consult the following (if you roll the result of a chamber that was shattered, there is no alchemical effect):</p><table><thead><tr><th>1d6</th><th>Alchemical Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><p>Acid Damage</p></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Cold Damage</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Electricity Damage</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Fire Damage</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Poison Damage</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Sickness: DC 26 Fortitude save or Sickened 1 (Sickened 2 on a critical failure)</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"n":"Alchemical Rupture","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a noxious needler takes physical damage from a critical hit or is affected by a shatter spell, one glass chamber within its body shatters, spewing alchemical liquid in a 5-foot emanation. Roll on the alchemical chambers list to determine which one shatters—on a roll of 1–5, creatures in the area take 10d6 damage of the appropriate type (DC 28 basic Reflex). On a roll of 6, creatures must instead save against the sickness effect.</p><table><thead><tr><th>1d6</th><th>Alchemical Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><p>10d6 acid damage DC 28 basic Reflex</p></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>10d6 cold damage DC 28 basic Reflex</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>10d6 electricity damage DC 28 basic Reflex</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>10d6 fire damage DC 28 basic Reflex</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>10d6 poison damage DC 28 basic Reflex</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Sickness: DC 26 Fortitude save or Sickened 1 (Sickened 2 on a critical failure)</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"n":"Alchemical Injection","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a noxious needler hits a creature with a syringe Strike, roll 1d6 on the alchemical chambers list to determine the additional effect of the attack. The syringe deals an additional 2d6 damage of the appropriate type (or exposes the target to the sickness effect, as appropriate).</p><table><thead><tr><th>1d6</th><th>Alchemical Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><p>2d6 acid damage</p></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2d6 cold damage</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2d6 electricity damage</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2d6 fire damage</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2d6 poison damage</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Sickness: DC 26 Fortitude save or Sickened 1 (Sickened 2 on a critical failure)</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"n":"Attach Siphon","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature that takes any damage from a blood siphon Strike must attempt a DC 28 Reflex save. On a failure, the siphon attaches to the creature. It's attached to Junth by a long length of flexible leather hose, so if he misses with a blood siphon he can retract it as an Interact action. The tether can also be severed (AC 27, Hardness 10, 10 HP), and the creature to which it has been attached can Escape.</p>"},{"n":"Siphon Blood","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> Junth has a blood siphon attached to a target creature</p>\n<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Junth drains blood from the target creature, dealing 5d6 bleed damage. Unlike most bleed damage, this isn't persistent damage. A creature that takes any of this bleed damage must also attempt a DC 28 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature takes no damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and becomes Enfeebled 1 for 1 hour.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage, becomes Enfeebled 2 for 1 hour, and becomes Drained 1. If the creature is already drained, it instead increases the value of its drained condition by 1.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/korven-willachur":{"lang":["Common","Varisian"],"lore":[["Bastardhall Lore",15]],"notes":{"hp":"rejuvenation, void healing"},"abilities":[{"n":"Site Bound","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Area <strong>C28</strong></p>\n<p>A typical ghost can stray only a short distance from where it was killed or the place it haunts. A typical limit is 120 feet. Some ghosts are instead bound to a room, building, item, or creature that was special to it rather than a location.</p>"},{"n":"Rejuvenation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>When a ghost is destroyed, it re-forms after 2d4 days within the location it's bound to, fully healed. A ghost can be permanently destroyed only if someone determines the reason for its existence and sets right whatever prevents the spirit from resting.</p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Compelling Order","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","divine","emotion","linguistic","mental"],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Korven barks out an order at a creature within 30 feet, filling his command with humiliating insults and implied threats of punishment if the order is disobeyed, choosing from one of the following commands: \"drop your weapons,\" \"kneel,\" \"stand at attention,\" or \"pledge your loyalty.\" The target must attempt a DC 26 Will save with the following results, after which it is immune to Compelling Order for 1 minute.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature can ignore the order; doing so causes Korven to sputter and rage, causing him to become Slowed 1 on his next turn.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature resists the order but takes 4d6 mental damage as it does so.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is compelled to spend its first action on its next turn following the order, and also takes 8d6 mental damage from the shame.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is compelled to spend its entire turn following the order, and takes 16d6 mental damage from the shame.</p>"},{"n":"Phantom Amputation","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>Each time a creature takes damage in a round from the bloody tendril Strike extending from Korven's wrist, it believes its fingers, hand, or similar appendage has been amputated. The creature must succeed at a DC 26 Will save or become Frightened 1 (Frightened 2 on a critical failure). If the creature is already frightened, it instead becomes Clumsy 1 for 24 hours on a failed save (or Clumsy 2 for 24 hours on critical failure).</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/lavinia-bavah":{"lang":["Common"],"lore":[["Dwelling Lore (applies to the place the ghost is bound to)",10]],"notes":{"hp":"rejuvenation, void healing"},"flavor":"<p>The ghost commoner is an ordinary person who believes they died unjustly.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>When some mortals die through tragic circumstances or without closure, their souls can linger and haunt a locale significant to them in life.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Site Bound","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A typical ghost can stray only a short distance from where it was killed or the place it haunts. A typical limit is 120 feet. Some ghosts are instead bound to a room, building, item, or creature that was special to it rather than a location.</p>"},{"n":"Rejuvenation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>Setting right the injustice that led to the commoner's death allows it to move on to the afterlife.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>When a ghost is destroyed, it re-forms after 2d4 days within the location it's bound to, fully healed. A ghost can be permanently destroyed only if someone determines the reason for its existence and sets right whatever prevents the spirit from resting.</p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Haunted by the Night","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>After dark, Lavinia loses all reason and becomes overtaken by the curse. She loses the ability to speak and attacks the PCs on sight. While haunted by the night, Lavinia is Quickened, and can use the extra action to make a ghostly hand Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Infect Shame","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","emotion","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>Lavinia stares at a creature she can see within 30 feet, and transfers her overwhelming shame into that creature's mind. That creature takes 3d6 mental damage and must attempt a DC 22 Will save with the following effects.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> No effect and temporarily immune for 1 minute.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage, and is Slowed 1 for 1 round as they agonize internally over its shame.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but double damage and slowed 1 for 1 minute.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-7-the-fallen-temple/lerrisu":{"lang":["Common"],"abilities":[{"n":"Site Bound","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>These three ghosts can only leave the crypts when Bastardhall appears on Golarion, and even then, they can't leave the island.</p>"},{"n":"Rejuvenation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>The three adventurers re-form after 2d4 days here in the crypts unless someone carries their respective urn of ashes down to the temple in Maiserene (area <strong>A9</strong>) to be cleansed by Shirail.</p>"},{"n":"Lerrisu's Dirge","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","divine","emotion","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>Lerrisu begins to sing a mournful dirge that unsettles the living but bolsters her ghostly allies. Each living creature within 30 feet must attempt a DC 28 Will save with the following results, while each of her ghostly companions within 30 feet become Quickened until the start of Lerrisu's next turn; they can use the extra action only to Fly or Strike.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target is unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The target is Off-Guard by sadness invoked by the dirge until the start of Lerrisu's next turn.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> As success, but also Slowed 1 by sadness.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but Slowed 2.</p>"},{"n":"Malevolent Possession","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["incapacitation","mental","possession"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ghost attempts to possess an adjacent corporeal creature. This has the same effect as the Possession spell (DC 23 Will), except since the ghost doesn't have a physical body, it is unaffected by that restriction of the spell.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-5-the-dungeon/let-my-torments-be-yours":{"description":"<p>The skeleton suddenly appears to lurch forward, crying out in pain as a shaking voice begs for someone to take away the pain.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 32 Thievery (master) to carefully remove the Vile Remnant from the skeleton's ribcage without triggering the haunt or DC 35 Religion (expert) to exorcise the haunt</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(master)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"A Gift of Torment","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":["curse","divine"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature attacks the skeleton or ends their turn adjacent to the remains</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The skeleton lurches to unlife just long enough to beg for peace, then exhales at the triggering creature, who must attempt a DC 33 Will save or be subjected to the haunt's curse. A cursed creature becomes racked with pain from what feels like hours spent under a torturer's knives and takes a –1 status penalty to Fortitude saves. In addition, each time the creature takes slashing or piercing damage, they must succeed at a DC 33 Fortitude save or become Sickened 1 from the agony (Sickened 2 on a critical failure). The curse remains until removed by Cleanse Affliction or similar magic.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-4-the-reception-halls/madam-skerzana":{"lang":["Aklo","Chthonian","Common","Petran"],"lore":[["Fortune Lore",20]],"gear":["Silver Hand-Mirror"],"abilities":[{"n":"Mirrosight","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>As long as Madam Skerzana holds a hand mirror, she can see through the mirror with darkvision and the effects of truesight. She's blind when she isn't holding a mirror.</p>"},{"n":"Light Sickness","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A stygira in an area of bright light is Sickened 1.</p>"},{"n":"Mirror Gaze","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>Madam Skerzana holds aloft her antique silver mirror and gazes through it into the past or future of a creature within 30 feet, infusing its thoughts with visions of their helplessness as a new-born baby or of a potential death from violence or peril in the future. Madam Skerzana chooses if she's showing the past or the future when she uses Mirror Gaze. The targeted creature must attempt a DC 26 Will save or become affected as indicated below, depending on which vision Madam Skerzana chose to impart.</p>\n<p><strong>The Past</strong> A glowing green eye momentarily opens on the right side of Madam Skerzana's face, and the target creature becomes Stupefied 1 (Stupefied 2 on a critical failure) as their mind reverts to a limited infantile intellect. At the end of the character's turn, the value of this stupefied condition decreases by 1.</p>\n<p><strong>The Future</strong> A glowing blue eye momentarily opens on the left side of Madam Skerzana's face, and the target creature becomes Frightened 1 (Frightened 2 on a critical failure) as their mind reels from a harrowing vision of their imminent death. Describe this death vision to the PC briefly, choosing one of the Arudoras that the party has yet to encounter and defeat. When that PC encounters that Arudora later in the campaign, they must attempt a new DC 26 Will save to avoid becoming frightened 1 (frightened 2 on a critical failure) all over again.</p>"},{"n":"Stone Curse","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["curse","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>Wounds dealt by the stygira's claws leave the flesh bleached of color and turn the blood that runs from them dark gray. Each time a creature is damaged by the stygira's claw Strike, it must succeed at a DC 26 Fortitude save or become permanently Slowed 1 (Slowed 2 on a critical failure) as its flesh stiffens like stone. If a creature is reduced to 0 Hit Points from the stygira's claw Strike and fails the saving throw against stone curse, it's petrified. A creature that spends 8 hours in direct sunlight can attempt a new saving throw to remove the effects of stone curse, even if it has been petrified.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/adventure-toolbox/middenworm":{"flavor":"<p>Creepers of sewers and crawlers within urban dump sites, middenworms are dangerous scavengers. Eager to abandon their bottom-feeding ways, they're known to attack living foes, for fresh blood is their favorite repast. A middenworm is unpleasant to look at, listen to, or smell: a greasy maggot-like worm with hooked jaws at one end that peel back the folds of its body when it bites, and a nest of long, red, blood-drinking tendrils at the other.</p><h2>Giant Middenworms</h2><p>Normally, a middenworm doesn’t grow much larger than a dog, as this smaller size affords them more mobility in the sewers they frequent. Yet rarely, when middenworms have more room to grow in an area that’s particularly infused with toxic magic or fiendish influences, these creatures can grow to enormous size.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Nauseating Presence","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. DC 19 Fortitude</p>\n<p>As stench, but the aura affects a creature that sees, hears, or smells the middenworm.</p><hr><p><p>A creature entering the aura or starting its turn in the area must succeed at a Fortitude save or become Sickened 1 (plus Slowed 1 as long as it's sickened on a critical failure). A creature that succeeds at its save or recovers from being sickened is temporarily immune to all stench auras for 1 minute.</p></p>"},{"n":"Blood Drain","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> A Grabbed, Paralyzed, Restrained, Unconscious, or willing creature is within reach of the middenworm's tails</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The middenworm drains blood from the victim, which must succeed at a DC 22 Fortitude save or become Drained 1. If the creature is already drained, its drained value increases by 1 (to a maximum of drained 4). The middenworm becomes Quickened until the end of its next turn, and can use the extra action to Stride, Climb, Swim, or Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Midden Fever","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["disease"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 22 Fortitude</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 1</strong> carrier with no ill effects (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 2</strong> Enfeebled 1 (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 3</strong> Enfeebled 2 (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 4</strong> enfeebled 2 and Fatigued (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 5</strong> Paralyzed (1 day)</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/adventure-toolbox/middenworm-infestation":{"description":"<p>A dead body seems to ripple and bulge, as if something inside were shaking.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 23 Medicine or DC 26 Thievery to carefully cut into the corpse and vent the worms harmlessly in a safe direction</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Worm Burst","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature jostles or touches the infested corpse</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The corpse bursts open, spraying hundreds of middenworm larvae in a 20-foot emanation. Creatures in the area must attempt a DC 25 Reflex save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Sickened 1 and takes 2d8 piercing damage from worm bites.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is sickened 1 and takes 4d8 piercing damage plus 2d6 persistent piercing damage as the biting worms latch on.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but Sickened 2 and the persistent piercing damage increases to 4d6 persistent piercing damage.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/milsindi-mopsindur":{"lang":["Common","Gnomish","Necril","Wildsong"],"lore":[["Carpentry Lore",17],["Labor Lore",15]],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"abilities":[{"n":"Stench","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","olfactory"],"f":"","d":"<p>10 feet. DC 23 Fortitude</p><hr><p><p>A creature entering the aura or starting its turn in the area must succeed at a Fortitude save or become Sickened 1 (plus Slowed 1 as long as it's sickened on a critical failure). A creature that succeeds at its save or recovers from being sickened is temporarily immune to all stench auras for 1 minute.</p></p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Consume Flesh","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The ghoul is adjacent to the corpse of a creature that died within the last hour.</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul devours a chunk of the corpse and regains 5d6 healing Hit Points.</p>\n<p>It can regain Hit Points from any given corpse only once.</p>"},{"n":"Ghoul Whispers","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","linguistic","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> A Grabbed, Paralyzed, Restrained, or Unconscious creature is within the ghoul's reach</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul whispers dark thoughts and vile cravings into the creature's ears. The creature must save against the forbidden cravings curse.</p>\n<p><strong>Forbidden Cravings</strong> (curse) A creature can still eat and drink while sickened by this curse</p>\n<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 26 Will</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 1</strong> carrier with no ill effects (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 2d6 void damage and the target is Sickened 1 until it consumes raw meat (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 3</strong> as stage 2</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 4</strong> as stage 2 unless the target has consumed raw meat in the past 24 hours, then it takes 4d6 void damage and is Sickened 2 until it consumes raw meat;</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 5</strong> if the creature has eaten raw meat in the past 24 hours, it dies and rises as a ghoul, if not, it returns to stage 4</p>"},{"n":"Grave Knowledge","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["occult"],"f":"once per hour","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per hour</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul calls upon knowledge it retains from one creature it has consumed in the past 7 days. The ghoul attempts a skill check using a skill in which the consumed creature was trained (if it's unclear whether the creature was trained, the GM decides). The ghoul is treated as trained with a +18 skill modifier. This takes the same amount of actions or time as usual for the check.</p>\n<p>The ghoul can instead automatically learn something specific known by a creature it consumed in the last 7 days, like the location of a Hidden treasure or the name of a loved one. The ghoul can do this only once for a given creature, no matter how much of its flesh the ghoul consumed.</p>"},{"n":"Swift Leap","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["move"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ghoul jumps up to half its Speed. This movement doesn't trigger reactions.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-4-the-reception-halls/mister-moxie":{"lang":["Common","Necril","Varisian"],"lore":[["Games Lore",14]],"gear":["Whip","Illuminated Harrow Card Deck","Pouch of dozens of different colored glass beads"],"abilities":[{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Change of Luck","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":["occult"],"f":"once per day","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per day</p>\n<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 30 feet of the bone croupier attempts a check but hasn't rolled the check yet</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The bone croupier pulls on the threads of luck that bind all things. The target rolls twice, and then the bone croupier decides which result applies. If the bone croupier chooses the lower roll, this is a misfortune effect; if the bone croupier chooses the higher roll, it's a fortune effect; if they're the same, the croupier decides which trait to apply. If this ability is expended, the bone croupier rolls 2d6 at the start of each of their turns, recharging the ability if the sum is 7 or 11.</p>"},{"n":"Pick a Card, Any Card","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>Mister Moxie designates an adjacent living creature who must pick a card from his deck of harrow cards. The chosen creature finds a phantasmal duplicate deck of cards in their hand and is compelled by fate to draw a card; it can and must do so even if its hands are full, or it's unable to act. The creature rolls 1d6 to determine the suit of the card drawn to determine the effect (or if you have a harrow deck handy, have the player draw a card to determine the suit).</p>\n<p><strong>1—Hammer:</strong> (void) The target becomes Enfeebled 1 for 24 hours.</p>\n<p><strong>2—Key:</strong> The target becomes Quickened for 1 minute and can use the extra action each round to Stride or Strike.</p>\n<p><strong>3—Shield:</strong> (void) The target becomes Drained 1.</p>\n<p><strong>4—Tome:</strong> (void) The target becomes Stupefied 1 for 24 hours.</p>\n<p><strong>5—Star:</strong> (healing, vitality) The target regains 20 healing vitality HP.</p>\n<p><strong>6—Crown:</strong> (emotion, mental) The target becomes Confused for 1 minute.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/adventure-toolbox/mymnian":{"lang":["Chthonian","Common","Diabolic","Empyrean","Requian","telepathy 100 feet"],"flavor":"<p>A mymnian enjoys the unique fear and foolish, panic-induced acts that mortals display when confronted with what they fear to be ghosts. Be it a mymnian that uses their own abilities to mimic a haunted abode or one that settles into an area with actual ghosts, a haunted house under the watchful eye of one of these manipulative sahkils is never a safe place for the living. Typically, a mymnian wears what appears to be a billowing gray cloak to look like an oversized humanoid figure, only to cast aside the cloak to reveal that their head and torso are a mass of humanoid and animal skulls, melted into a hideous conglomeration with each bony face looking outward in different directions. They walk upon five long skeletal arms that end in bony, humanlike hands. And that cloak? Nothing less unsettling than long, rippling folds of leathery flesh that hang from their body like a cape.</p><h2>Eerie Rumors</h2><p>Towns that host a haunted house would expect most people to steer clear of them, but there's always a reason for someone new to brave the terrors. Rumors might spread about lost treasures, some might dare friends to \"just spend one night,\" or others might seek to learn tantalizing secrets from the beyond; whatever the form, these lures are cultivated by a mymnian, eager to draw new victims into their playground.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Easy to Call","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A mymnian's level is considered 2 lower for the purpose of being conjured by the Binding Circle ritual (and potentially other rituals, at the GM's discretion), but it is always free to attack or leave instead of negotiate unless the primary caster's check is a critical success.</p>"},{"n":"Watchful Presence","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","divine","emotion","fear","mental","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>60 feet. Even when a mymnian doesn't notice you, you feel like they—or something worse—are watching. A creature that begins its turn inside the aura feels an overwhelming sense of being watched, gaining a +2 status bonus to Perception checks but taking a –2 penalty to saving throws against fear effects. A creature in the aura can't reduce the value of its Frightened condition below 1.</p>\n<p>Effect: Watchful Presence</p>"},{"n":"Doomed to Rise","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature that dies from an attack by a mymnian while frightened becomes a ghost within the next 24 hours.</p>"},{"n":"Extract Ghost","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["divine","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The mymnian attempts a claw Strike. If it hits, it deals no damage—instead, the mymnian's claws hook onto the target's soul and, for a moment, tug it out of the body so that the victim can catch a glimpse of how it will look after it dies. The target takes 5d8 mental damage with a DC 33 Will save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target takes no damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The target takes half damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The target takes full damage and becomes Frightened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The target takes double damage and becomes Frightened 2.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-4-the-reception-halls/no-light-shall-guide-thee":{"description":"<p>The <em>evercursed crystals</em> suddenly extinguish if they are disturbed, taking the PCs' sight away with their failing light.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 30 Thievery (master) to subtly sift the ashes aside in a way where the curse simply fades without discerning a target or DC 33 Occultism (expert) to know how to harmlessly disperse the malign magic</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(master)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Devour Sight","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["curse","incapacitation","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature disturbs the ashes in the fireplace or attempts to manipulate any of the evercursed crystals</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The crystals burn out, becoming non-magical stones, and the triggering creature must attempt a DC 32 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature's vision blurs and dims, and they become Dazzled for 24 hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> As success, but with an unlimited duration.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Blinded with an unlimited duration.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-2-castle-grounds/nulferion":{"flavor":"<p>Horses serve as mounts and beasts of burden in many societies. They are loyal and typically gentle creatures, and they are invaluable to those looking to travel long distances. Smaller folk, like gnomes and halflings, often utilize ponies as mounts, while horses are the favored steeds for humans and other Medium humanoids. Most horses that the average humanoid encounters are domesticated, though large herds can be found in the wild.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Buck","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>DC 17 Reflex</p>\n<p><hr>\n<p>Most monsters that serve as mounts can attempt to buck off unwanted or annoying riders, but most mounts will not use this reaction against a trusted creature unless the mounts are spooked or mistreated.</p>\n<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature Mounts or uses the Command an Animal action while riding the monster.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The triggering creature must succeed at a Reflex saving throw against the listed DC or fall off the creature and land Prone. If the save is a critical failure, the triggering creature also takes 1d6 bludgeoning damage in addition to the normal damage for the fall.</p></p>"},{"n":"Gallop","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The war horse Strides twice. It has a +10-foot circumstance bonus to its Speed during these Strides.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/phantom-knight-bastion-of-blasphemies":{"lang":["Common"],"notes":{"saves":"-1 status to all saves vs. death effects"},"flavor":"<p>Cavaliers and knights who died for their cause make for particularly strongwilled phantoms. Though their motives vary, these phantoms often seek to continue their lifelong missions even in their purgatorial states, and their strong forces of will make them formidable fighters in spite of their incorporeality. A phantom knight might strive to find living followers of their church or order, or seek out other mortals they knew in life as a way of staying grounded and avoiding the corruption of undeath.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>The typical trajectory for souls passing to the afterlife is fairly straightforward, according to most theologians. When a mortal dies, their soul enters the River of Souls and eventually reaches the Boneyard, where it is judged by Pharasma. The judged soul moves onto its appropriate domain of final rest—Heaven, Hell, Abaddon, and so forth—where it becomes a shade.</p>\n<p>Complications arise, however, when a soul in queue for judgment prematurely departs from the River of Souls and is shunted into the Ethereal Plane. Whether as a result of nefarious interlopers like daemons or hags, malignant planar magic, or even fate, these souls become dislodged from the natural order of life and death and linger in a sort of purgatory. Unlike petitioners, these ethereal phantoms retain memories of their life before death, and unlike spirits such as ghosts, phantoms aren't tinged with the foul influences of undeath—at least, not at first, though the threat of corruption hangs heavy over a phantom's existence. Some eventually succumb to that fate, while others eventually rejoin the River of Souls. Until then, these wandering souls are a kind unto themselves—one without a true home, agenda, or purpose.</p>\n<p>Many phantoms have no desire to remain in their strange state of purgatory, either because they seek to continue their journey through the River of Souls or because they fear the corruption of undeath. In order to complete the natural spiritual cycle and become judged so they can continue to the afterlife, a phantom must find a way back into the River of Souls. Such a quest is no easy feat, however—the hazy mists of the Ethereal Plane can befuddle even the most experienced traveler, and numerous predators prowl the realm in search of stray souls to bind or devour.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Bound to Bastardhall","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Unlike the typical phantom, these phantoms exist fully in the Ethereal Plane and cannot shift back and forth between this plane and the Universe.</p>"},{"n":"-1 Status to All Saves vs. Death Effects","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Susceptible to Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Though phantoms aren't alive, neither are they undead, and they are uniquely vulnerable to the effects of death.</p>\n<p>A phantom whose Hit Points are reduced to 0 as a result of a death effect (such as from a spell like Execute) is immediately whisked away to the River of Souls, where their soul resumes the usual path to the afterlife.</p>"},{"n":"Phantom Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["spirit"],"f":"","d":"<p>Each time they make a Strike, a phantom can choose to deal spirit damage instead of the normal physical damage type.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/poisoned-skull":{"description":"<p>A fast-acting toxin that's almost invisible to the eye coats the first and seventh skull on this display shelf.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 31 Thievery to identify the toxin and delicately wipe it free before burning the tainted cloth; on a critical success, the character can pinpoint two areas where the skull can be safely carried and, perhaps, used as an improvised thrown weapon to poison a creature.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(expert)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Stain and Pain","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature comes in contact with the skull</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The contact poison turns the exposed flesh a deep scarlet color that persists for 24 hours and also afflicts the creature with nettleweed residue.</p><hr><p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 27 Fortitude</p>\n<p><strong>Onset</strong> 1 minute</p>\n<p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 minutes</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 1</strong> 3d6 poison damage (1 minute)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 4d6 poison damage (1 minute)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 3</strong> 6d6 poison damage (1 minute)</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-6-the-private-halls/raudltz-arudora":{"lang":["Common","Hallit","Necril"],"gear":["Leather Armor","Key Ring","Shadow Signet","Raudltz's Telescope"],"notes":{"hp":"coffin restoration, fast healing 10, void healing"},"abilities":[{"n":"Children of the Night","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["divine","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>Raudltz's ability to issue telepathic orders extends throughout the private halls and can be issued to any of his vampiric \"children.\"</p><hr><p>The vampire's presence brings forth creatures of the night to do the master's bidding. These typically include Rat Swarms, Bat Swarms, and Wolves, but can include other creatures.</p>\n<p>The vampire can give telepathic orders to these creatures as long as they are within 100 feet, but they can't communicate back.</p>"},{"n":"Fast Healing 10","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A monster with this ability regains the given number of Hit Points each round at the beginning of its turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Coffin Restoration","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine","void"],"f":"","d":"<p>Unlike other undead, a vampire isn't destroyed at 0 HP. Instead, it falls Unconscious. If its body rests in its coffin for 1 hour, the vampire gains 1 HP, after which its fast healing begins to function normally.</p>"},{"n":"Mist Escape","a":"f","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The vampire is reduced to 0 HP.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The vampire uses Turn to Mist. They can take move actions to move toward its coffin even though it's at 0 HP. While at 0 HP in this form, the vampire is unaffected by further damage.</p>\n<p>They automatically returns to its corporeal form, unconscious, if they reaches their coffin or after 2 hours, whichever comes first.</p>"},{"n":"Vampire Vulnerabilities","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>As vampire, but Raudltz is particularly sensitive if anyone even hints at the idea that not all of his children are legitimate heirs to the Arudora fortune and name. Any attempt to Demoralize or Feint against Raudltz that incorporates the linguistic trait and taunts him about rumors of illegitimate children gain a +2 circumstance bonus to the check. On a success, Raudltz is Stupefied 1 for 1 round in addition to the other effects (stupefied 1 for 1 minute on a critical success). If he has any dominated PCs at this time, each dominated PC can attempt a new saving throw against Dominate to end the effect early, but no more often than once per round.</p><hr><p>All vampires possess the following vulnerabilities.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Compulsions</strong> Vampires are creatures with strange and unknowable compulsions. A typical vampire can't voluntarily cross running water unless they're transported while they hide within their coffin, nor can they enter a private dwelling unless invited in by someone with the authority to do so. At your discretion, vampires might have different compulsions—a pirate vampire might not be able to set foot on solid ground without being invited, for example. The vampire can still be forced to do these things and might be able to overcome their compulsion just as they do their revulsion.</p></li><li><p><strong>Revulsion</strong> A vampire can't voluntarily come within 10 feet of brandished garlic or a brandished religious symbol of a deity with a holy sanctification option. To brandish garlic or a religious symbol, a creature must Interact to do so, and it remains brandished for 1 round (similar to Raising a Shield). If the vampire involuntarily comes within 10 feet of an object of their revulsion, they gain the Fleeing condition, running from the object of their revulsion until they end an action beyond 10 feet. After 1 round of being exposed to the subject of their revulsion, a vampire can attempt a DC 25 Will save as a single action, which has the concentrate trait. On a success, they overcome their revulsions for 1d6 rounds (or 1 hour on a critical success).</p></li><li><p><strong>Stake</strong> A wooden stake driven through a vampire's heart drops the vampire to 0 HP and prevents them from healing above 0 HP, even in their coffin. Staking a vampire requires 3 actions and works only if the vampire is Unconscious. If the stake is removed, the vampire can heal above 0 HP again, and if they're in their coffin, the 1-hour rest period begins once the stake is removed. If the vampire's head is severed and anointed with holy water while the stake is in place, the vampire is destroyed.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sunlight</strong> If exposed to direct sunlight, a vampire immediately becomes Slowed 1. The slowed value increases by 1 each time the vampire ends their turn in sunlight, and the condition ends when they're no longer in sunlight. If the vampire loses all their actions in this way, they're destroyed. Due to their supernatural aversion to light, vampires don't cast shadows or show a reflection in mirrors</p></li></ul>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Change Shape","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","divine","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>Giant bat with fangs +24 for 3d8+16 piercing</p><hr><p><p>The monster changes its shape indefinitely. It can use this action again to return to its natural shape or adopt a new shape. Unless otherwise noted, a monster cannot use Change Shape to appear as a specific individual. Using Change Shape counts as creating a disguise for the Impersonate use of Deception. The monster's transformation automatically defeats Perception DCs to determine whether the creature is a member of the ancestry or creature type into which it transformed, and it gains a +4 status bonus to its Deception DC to prevent others from seeing through its disguise. Change Shape abilities specify what shapes the monster can adopt. The monster doesn't gain any special abilities of the new shape, only its physical form. For example, in each shape, it replaces its normal Speeds and Strikes, and might potentially change its senses or size. Any changes are listed in its stat block.</p></p>"},{"n":"Create Servitor","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine","downtime"],"f":"","d":"<p>If a creature dies after being reduced to 0 HP by Drink Blood, the vampire can turn this victim into a vampire servitor by donating some of their own blood to the victim and burying the victim in earth for 3 nights.</p>\n<p>A vampiric servitor is compelled to obey its creator, but if a vampire controls too many vampiric servitors at once (as determined by the GM), or if the servitor is a higher level than the vampire that created it, strong-willed servitors can free themselves by succeeding at a Will vs. Will DC saving throw against the vampire's Will DC.</p>"},{"n":"Dominate","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","incapacitation","mental","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>The vampire can cast Dominate at will as a divine innate spell. Casting it requires staring into the target's eyes, giving the spell the visual trait (Will). The save DC uses the high spell DC of the vampire's level, and a creature that succeeds is temporarily immune to that vampire's Dominate for 24 hours.</p>\n<p>Fully destroying the vampire ends the domination, but merely reducing the vampire to 0 HP is insufficient to break the spell.</p>"},{"n":"Drink Blood","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirement</strong> A Grabbed, Paralyzed, Restrained, Unconscious, or willing creature is within the vampire's reach.</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The vampire sinks its fangs into that creature to drink its blood. This requires an Athletics vs. Fortitude DC check against the victim's Fortitude DC if the victim is grabbed and is automatic for any of the other conditions.</p>\n<p>The victim is Drained 2 and the vampire regains 16 healing HP, gaining any excess HP as temporary Hit Points. Drinking Blood from a creature that's already drained doesn't restore any HP to the vampire but increases the victim's drained value by 1, killing the victim when it reaches drained 5. A vampire can also consume blood that's been emptied into a vessel for sustenance, but it gains no HP from doing so.</p>\n<p>A victim's drained condition decreases by 1 per week. A blood transfusion, which requires a DC 20 Medicine check and sufficient blood or a blood donor, reduces the drain by 1 after 10 minutes.</p>"},{"n":"Turn to Mist","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["air","concentrate","divine","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>The vampire turns into a cloud of vapor, as the Vapor Form spell, or back to its normal form. The vampire loses fast healing while in gaseous form.</p>\n<p>The vampire can remain in this form indefinitely.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-1-the-haunted-shore/rembles-worthy":{"focus":1,"flavor":"<p>Barghests are canine beasts that take great joy in the hunt, often lurking near humanoid settlements to find prey that can provide a suitable challenge. Even in the deepest wilderness, barghests choose the most clever or difficult prey, searching endlessly for challenges like giants, nymphs, and unicorns.</p>\n<p>More than victory or even the possibility of a meal at the end, barghests enjoy the thrill of the chase and the fear they create within their prey. Particularly terrified targets of a hunting barghest might even be allowed to escape, spreading the terror and enticing hunters who can become the barghest's next victims. A handful of barghests resort to learning magical methods of causing fear directly, though they find such fear hollow and unsatisfying.</p>\n<p>Although they rarely tolerate the competition of another barghest's presence, these hunters will happily work with anyone who helps them find prey to torment and kill. This often involves enforcing the will of hags or particularly cruel fey, but a bored barghest might also force a family of weaker humanoids to work as scouts and bait.</p>\n<p>Typical barghests often make use of their shapechanging abilities to hide in plain sight, only taking their true forms to frighten their prey and exult in the hunt. However, they are often still exposed by their trail of victims or the curiously unhealing wounds they inflict, even in humanoid form. Tales of powerful barghests with invisible heads are sometimes told in remote and dwindling villages. These barghests generally dispense with hiding as a humanoid or dog, instead terrifying entire communities and openly hunting increasingly dangerous prey. Some also extend this pride to their intellect, insisting on matching wits with their prey or even with those who believe they're hunting the barghest.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Primal Hunt","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["primal","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the barghest's reach takes a move or teleportation action</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> After the triggering action, the barghest can teleport up to 60 feet to a space adjacent to that creature.</p>"},{"n":"Change Shape","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","polymorph","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The barghest takes on the shape of a humanoid, a dog, or its true form. Their size changes to match the new form. When the barghest is a humanoid, their claw Strike deals bludgeoning damage and they lose their jaws Strike. When the barghest is a dog, their Speed changes to 35 feet. Each individual barghest has only one humanoid form and one dog form.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><p>The monster changes its shape indefinitely. It can use this action again to return to its natural shape or adopt a new shape. Unless otherwise noted, a monster cannot use Change Shape to appear as a specific individual. Using Change Shape counts as creating a disguise for the Impersonate use of Deception. The monster's transformation automatically defeats Perception DCs to determine whether the creature is a member of the ancestry or creature type into which it transformed, and it gains a +4 status bonus to its Deception DC to prevent others from seeing through its disguise. Change Shape abilities specify what shapes the monster can adopt. The monster doesn't gain any special abilities of the new shape, only its physical form. For example, in each shape, it replaces its normal Speeds and Strikes, and might potentially change its senses or size. Any changes are listed in its stat block.</p></p>"},{"n":"Isolating Howl","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","emotion","incapacitation","mental","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>Rembles lets loose an unearthly howl filled with utter despair. All creatures other than barghests within a 60 foot emanation who hear the howl become filled with the sense that they're all alone in the world, and that an impossible distance spans between themselves and anything remotely connected to companionship. Affected creatures must attempt a DC 24 Will save with the following effects.</p>\n<p>Rembles can't use Isolating Howl again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> Convictions of loneliness fill the target's mind, and they cannot target allies or take the Aid action for 1 round.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> As success, but the weight of being so alone crushes down on the creature, causing them to become Slowed 1 for 1 minute as well.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> There's no reason to try. The creature is Paralyzed from ennui and despair for 1 minute, but can attempt a DC 24 Will save at the end of each of their turns to break free from this despair—on a success, they're no longer paralyzed but become slowed 1 for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Unhealing Wound","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["curse","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature damaged by the barghest's claws must succeed at a DC 24 Fortitude save or be cursed. The cursed creature can't regain Hit Points except via magic until it returns to maximum Hit Points. The creature can attempt a new saving throw against the curse every 24 hours.</p>"},{"n":"Knockdown","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Knockdown in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Trip the creature. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-7-the-fallen-temple/ryhasphinea-arudora":{"lang":["Common","Daemonic","Necril"],"gear":["Ryhasphinea's Hourglass"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"abilities":[{"n":"Religious Disgust","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A character who openly wears religious iconography or the religious symbol of either Pharasma or Shelyn gains a +2 status bonus to their AC for attacks from Ryhasphinea. If a PC attempts to Demoralize Ryhasphinea by invoking either deity, they can attempt to Demoralize with a Religion check instead of an Intimidation check. If she becomes frightened by this attempt, she also becomes Slowed 1 as long as she remains frightened out of disgust.</p>"},{"n":"Share Blessings","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["disease","divine","unholy"],"f":"","d":"<p>When Ryhasphinea is destroyed, she explodes in a blast of infectious rot. All creatures in a 10-foot emanation are exposed to soulphage.</p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Feed the Dead","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","unholy"],"f":"once per minute","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per minute</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements</strong> Ryhasphinea is adjacent to an undead creature</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Ryhasphinea siphons void energy into the undead creature, granting it 30 temporary Hit Points and making it Quickened for 3 rounds. The undead creature's temporary Hit Points last as long as it remains quickened, and it can use the extra action only to Stride or Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Soulphage","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["disease","divine","unholy","virulent"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 33 Fortitude</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 1</strong> carrier (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 3d8 void damage and Drained 1 (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 3</strong> 4d8 void damage, drained 1, and Enfeebled 2 (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 4</strong> 4d8 void damage, drained 1, enfeebled 2, and Clumsy 3 (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 5</strong> 4d8 void damage, Drained 2, Enfeebled 3, clumsy 3, and Doomed 3</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 6</strong> dead. A creature that dies while infected with soulphage rises as a War Wraith</p>"},{"n":"Visceral Reap","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Ryhasphinea makes a scything blade Strike and compares the attack roll result to the AC of up to two foes, each of whom must be within her melee reach. Roll damage only once, and apply it to each creature hit. The first creature damaged by Visceral Reap also takes 1d6 persistent bleed damage. Reap counts as two attacks for Ryhasphinea's multiple attack penalty.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-1-the-haunted-shore/sahkil-imp":{"lang":["Common","Diabolic","Chthonian","Daemonic","Telepathy (Touch)"],"focus":1,"flavor":"<p>Imps are fiendish infiltrators and corrupters who, despite their diminutive stature, are more than capable of subtly influencing a weak-willed individual into performing increasingly evil acts over time. An imp will often agree to serve a mortal and act docile and loyal in a long-term plot to eventually get their master killed or damn their soul. Imps are born directly from the Outer Planes themselves, rather than from mortal souls, and thus they serve outside any fiendish hierarchies, granting them leeway to pursue their specialties. Despite standing a mere 2 feet tall, imps can be vicious combatants, flying out of reach and turning invisible to escape should the odds turn against them.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Telepathy (Touch)","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["aura","magical","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A monster with telepathy can communicate mentally with any creatures within the listed radius, as long as they share a language. This doesn't give any special access to their thoughts, and communicates no more information than normal speech would.</p></p>"},{"n":"Change Shape","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>The imp takes on the appearance of a Medium or smaller animal. While transformed, the imp loses their normal senses, innate spells, and special actions, but doesn't otherwise change their statistics and can still speak and use telepathy. The imp also gains any special senses of the animal and any Speeds the animal has.</p>\n<p>This doesn't change the attack and damage modifiers of their Strikes but might change the damage type their Strikes deal (depending on what kinds of attacks the animal has) and prevents them from exposing creatures to imp venom.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><p>The monster changes its shape indefinitely. It can use this action again to return to its natural shape or adopt a new shape. Unless otherwise noted, a monster cannot use Change Shape to appear as a specific individual. Using Change Shape counts as creating a disguise for the Impersonate use of Deception. The monster's transformation automatically defeats Perception DCs to determine whether the creature is a member of the ancestry or creature type into which it transformed, and it gains a +4 status bonus to its Deception DC to prevent others from seeing through its disguise. Change Shape abilities specify what shapes the monster can adopt. The monster doesn't gain any special abilities of the new shape, only its physical form. For example, in each shape, it replaces its normal Speeds and Strikes, and might potentially change its senses or size. Any changes are listed in its stat block.</p></p>"},{"n":"Fiendish Healing","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","divine","healing","vitality"],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The imp regains 1d6 healing Hit Points.</p>"},{"n":"Fiendish Temptation","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","divine","fortune","unholy"],"f":"once per day","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per day</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The imp offers a nonfiend within 15 feet a bargain, granting a boon of good luck if the creature accepts voluntarily. The boon lasts for 1 hour once accepted.</p>\n<p>Once during the hour, the creature can roll an attack roll or saving throw twice and use the higher result. If the creature dies while the boon is in place, the imp decides where the creature's soul travels. This typically makes the soul bound for eternity in the imp's home plane, and the creature unable to be raised or resurrected except by the Wish ritual or similar magic.</p>"},{"n":"Imp Venom","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 16 Fortitude</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 1</strong> 1d6 poison damage and Clumsy 1 (1 round)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 1d6 poison damage, clumsy 1, and Slowed 1 (1 round)</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-7-the-fallen-temple/sanctum-guardian":{"lang":["Common","Jotun","Necril","Daemonic"],"gear":["Scythe","Black Onyx Gems"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"flavor":"<p>Masters of mausoleums and keepers of crypts, the dread creatures called tomb giants are anathema to all living beings, but especially so to other types of giants. Long ago, so the legends say, tomb giants sold their souls in exchange for unfathomable power over the undead. Tomb giants construct massive gothic settlements in haunted valleys and on forsaken hillsides, far enough away from the societies of smaller people that they remain relatively undisturbed, but close enough that they can raid the graveyards of nearby villages with impunity. Elder tomb giants enjoy the thrill of subduing, slaying, and reanimating their fellow giants. A tomb giant views its mortal life as only one part of its existence. After death, most tomb giants are reanimated as undead, who then continue to practice their necromantic arts.</p><hr><p>Spread across the world, giants are as diverse as the isolated places they inhabit. A giant makes a big impression on the local environment, especially on smaller and weaker creatures.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Lifesense (Imprecise) 60 feet","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>Lifesense allows a monster to sense the vital essence of living and undead creatures within the listed range. The sense can distinguish between the vitality energy animating living creatures and the void energy animating undead creatures, much as sight distinguishes colors.</p></p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Dooming Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine","void"],"f":"","d":"<p>The tomb giant's claws carry the accursed power of their foul gods. A creature hit by the tomb giant's claw Strike becomes Doomed 1.</p>"},{"n":"Font of Death","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","unholy","void"],"f":"","d":"<p>The tomb giant turns the spiritual tide on a creature that has just died, temporarily transforming it into a volatile vessel powered by the Void. The tomb giant touches a creature that died within the past 24 hours, infusing its flesh and bone with void energy. Once during the next hour, the tomb giant can spend a single action (from any distance) to release this void from the corpse in an explosion that deals 10d8 void damage in a 15-foot burst (DC 32 basic Fortitude save); if not released before the end of the hour, the energy dissipates harmlessly. The tomb giant can't use Font of Death while a previous corpse remains infused.</p>"},{"n":"Pallid Curse","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":["curse","divine"],"f":"once per day","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per day</p>\n<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A sanctum guardian deals void damage on a creature within 30 feet</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The void damage scars the target's soul, infecting it with Urgathoa's presence. The target must attempt a DC 32 Fortitude save with the following effects.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target suffers no additional effect.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The target becomes Sickened 1 and gains weakness to void 5 until the end of its next turn.</p>\n<p>Effect: Pallid Curse (Success)</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The target becomes Sickened 2 and gains weakness to void 5; this weakness has an unlimited duration. If the target dies while this curse remains in effect, the curse ends, but the target automatically rises as an undead creature (the GM picks one of the same level as the target) that goes on to serve Urgathoa in its unlife. If the target is destroyed in its new undead form, it reverts to its original living form but is Dying 3.</p>\n<p>Effect: Pallid Curse (Failure)</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but weakness to void 10.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/servitor-phantom-brewer":{"lang":["Common"],"lore":[["Alcohol Lore",8]],"notes":{"saves":"-1 status penalty to all saves vs. death effects"},"abilities":[{"n":"Bound to Bastardhall","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Unlike the typical phantom, these phantoms exist fully in the Ethereal Plane and cannot shift back and forth between this plane and the Universe.</p>"},{"n":"-1 Status to All Saves vs. Death Effects","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Skull-Faced Servant","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A servitor phantom's head is that of an animal skull, as determined by their original role in the castle before the curse fell. This skull head grants the servitor phantom a horns or jaws Strike (as appropriate). Each type of skull is associated with a different Lore skill.</p>\n<p><strong>Brewers</strong> have deer skulls and Alcohol Lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Cooks</strong> have bird skulls and Cooking Lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Housekeepers</strong> have cat skulls and Labor Lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Valets</strong> have dog skulls and Labor Lore.</p>"},{"n":"Susceptible to Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Though phantoms aren't alive, neither are they undead, and they are uniquely vulnerable to the effects of death.</p>\n<p>A phantom whose Hit Points are reduced to 0 as a result of a death effect (such as from a spell like Execute) is immediately whisked away to the River of Souls, where their soul resumes the usual path to the afterlife.</p>"},{"n":"Phantom Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["spirit"],"f":"","d":"<p>Each time they make a Strike, a phantom can choose to deal spirit damage instead of the normal physical damage type.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/servitor-phantom-cook":{"lang":["Common"],"lore":[["Cooking Lore",8]],"notes":{"saves":"-1 status penalty to all saves vs. death effects"},"abilities":[{"n":"Bound to Bastardhall","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Unlike the typical phantom, these phantoms exist fully in the Ethereal Plane and cannot shift back and forth between this plane and the Universe.</p>"},{"n":"-1 Status to All Saves vs. Death Effects","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Skull-Faced Servant","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A servitor phantom's head is that of an animal skull, as determined by their original role in the castle before the curse fell. This skull head grants the servitor phantom a horns or jaws Strike (as appropriate). Each type of skull is associated with a different Lore skill.</p>\n<p><strong>Brewers</strong> have deer skulls and Alcohol Lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Cooks</strong> have bird skulls and Cooking Lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Housekeepers</strong> have cat skulls and Labor Lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Valets</strong> have dog skulls and Labor Lore.</p>"},{"n":"Susceptible to Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Though phantoms aren't alive, neither are they undead, and they are uniquely vulnerable to the effects of death.</p>\n<p>A phantom whose Hit Points are reduced to 0 as a result of a death effect (such as from a spell like Execute) is immediately whisked away to the River of Souls, where their soul resumes the usual path to the afterlife.</p>"},{"n":"Phantom Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["spirit"],"f":"","d":"<p>Each time they make a Strike, a phantom can choose to deal spirit damage instead of the normal physical damage type.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/servitor-phantom-housekeeper":{"lang":["Common"],"lore":[["Labor Lore",8]],"notes":{"saves":"-1 status penalty to all saves vs. death effects"},"abilities":[{"n":"Bound to Bastardhall","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Unlike the typical phantom, these phantoms exist fully in the Ethereal Plane and cannot shift back and forth between this plane and the Universe.</p>"},{"n":"-1 Status to All Saves vs. Death Effects","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Skull-Faced Servant","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A servitor phantom's head is that of an animal skull, as determined by their original role in the castle before the curse fell. This skull head grants the servitor phantom a horns or jaws Strike (as appropriate). Each type of skull is associated with a different Lore skill.</p>\n<p><strong>Brewers</strong> have deer skulls and Alcohol Lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Cooks</strong> have bird skulls and Cooking Lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Housekeepers</strong> have cat skulls and Labor Lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Valets</strong> have dog skulls and Labor Lore.</p>"},{"n":"Susceptible to Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Though phantoms aren't alive, neither are they undead, and they are uniquely vulnerable to the effects of death.</p>\n<p>A phantom whose Hit Points are reduced to 0 as a result of a death effect (such as from a spell like Execute) is immediately whisked away to the River of Souls, where their soul resumes the usual path to the afterlife.</p>"},{"n":"Phantom Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["spirit"],"f":"","d":"<p>Each time they make a Strike, a phantom can choose to deal spirit damage instead of the normal physical damage type.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/servitor-phantom-valet":{"lang":["Common"],"lore":[["Labor Lore",8]],"notes":{"saves":"-1 status penalty to all saves vs. death effects"},"abilities":[{"n":"Bound to Bastardhall","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Unlike the typical phantom, these phantoms exist fully in the Ethereal Plane and cannot shift back and forth between this plane and the Universe.</p>"},{"n":"-1 Status to All Saves vs. Death Effects","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Skull-Faced Servant","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A servitor phantom's head is that of an animal skull, as determined by their original role in the castle before the curse fell. This skull head grants the servitor phantom a horns or jaws Strike (as appropriate). Each type of skull is associated with a different Lore skill.</p>\n<p><strong>Brewers</strong> have deer skulls and Alcohol Lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Cooks</strong> have bird skulls and Cooking Lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Housekeepers</strong> have cat skulls and Labor Lore.</p>\n<p><strong>Valets</strong> have dog skulls and Labor Lore.</p>"},{"n":"Susceptible to Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Though phantoms aren't alive, neither are they undead, and they are uniquely vulnerable to the effects of death.</p>\n<p>A phantom whose Hit Points are reduced to 0 as a result of a death effect (such as from a spell like Execute) is immediately whisked away to the River of Souls, where their soul resumes the usual path to the afterlife.</p>"},{"n":"Phantom Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["spirit"],"f":"","d":"<p>Each time they make a Strike, a phantom can choose to deal spirit damage instead of the normal physical damage type.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/adventure-toolbox/shirail-animender":{"lang":["Common"],"abilities":[{"n":"Bound to Bastardhall","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Unlike the typical phantom, Shirail exists fully in the Ethereal Plane and cannot shift back and forth between this plane and the Universe.</p>"},{"n":"–1 Status Penalty to All Saves vs. Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Bound Even in Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>As phantom, but if Shirail is reduced to 0 Hit Points by any means, her soul does not return to the River of Souls. Instead, her soul is drawn into the soul vortex in the vault under Bastardhall (area <strong>I</strong>).</p>"},{"n":"Physical Manifestation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Upon reaching rank 1, Shirail manifests a semi-physical body. She loses the incorporeal trait, her immunities and resistances, and her fly Speed, and gains a land Speed of 25 feet. She has a Strength modifier of +2, an Athletics modifier of +9, an AC of 21, 75 HP, and divine innate spells. Her other statistics do not change.</p>"},{"n":"Phantom Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["spirit"],"f":"","d":"<p>Each time they make a Strike, a phantom can choose to deal spirit damage instead of the normal physical damage type.</p>"},{"n":"Spellcasting","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Once Shirail is rank 1 and has a physical body, she gains the following divine innate spells. As her rank increases, her occult spell DC and spell attack modifier increase, and she gains additional innate spells.</p>\n<p><strong>Divine Innate Spells</strong> DC 22, attack +14; <strong>3rd</strong> Cleanse Affliction, Heal; <strong>2nd</strong> Cleanse Affliction, Heal, Sound Body; <strong>1st</strong> Cleanse Cuisine, Heal, Spirit Link; <strong>Cantrips (3rd)</strong> Detect Magic, Divine Lance, Light, Read Aura, Vitality Lash</p>\n<p><strong>Rank 2</strong> DC 24, attack +16; <strong>4th</strong> Cleanse Affliction; <strong>3rd</strong> Locate. Her cantrips are heightened to 4th rank.</p>\n<p><strong>Rank 3</strong> DC 26, attack +18; <strong>5th</strong> Sound Body; <strong>4th</strong> Vital Beacon. Her cantrips are heightened to 5th rank.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/slaughtermay":{"lang":["Common","Goblin"],"gear":["Hook Sword","Hook Sword","Studded Leather Armor","Doubling Rings"],"abilities":[{"n":"Reactive Strike","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Grisly Gash","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> Slaughtermay's previous action was a hook sword Strike that damaged a foe</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Slaughtermay twists and then yanks her hook sword, ripping at the flesh of the creature she damaged with a hook sword on her previous action. That creature takes 1d6+5 bleed damage (DC 22 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Sneak Attack","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The creature's Strikes deal an additional 1d6 precision damage to Off-Guard creatures.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/adventure-toolbox/sludge-troll":{"lang":["Jotun"],"notes":{"hp":"regeneration 20 (deactivated by cold or fire)","speed":"flexible limbs"},"flavor":"<p>The lanky, greasy sludge troll always seems to look like they just slid out of a pool of noxious sewage or filthy swamp water, with their scaly hide dripping with oily fluids and crusty muck. While they possess the iconic underbite and tusks of the typical troll, their facial features are much more fishlike, with bulging eyes and fin-like ridges on their spine, brow, and forearms. In addition to possessing prodigious regenerative qualities, sludge trolls are immune to diseases, and as a result, they often wallow and lair in areas infested with sickness for additional defenses. Tunnels and sewage outflows are their favored places to lurk in ambush, particularly if these areas see lots of traffic from underworld denizens. Generally more stealthy and sneaky than the typical troll, a sludge troll roused to violence is as terrifying and impossible to ignore as any others of their kind.</p><h2>Sewer Dwellers</h2><p>While sludge trolls are often found dwelling in mires and bogs, they are also perfectly at home lurking in sewers and cisterns below urban areas. Their lanky limbs and ability to squeeze through smaller areas helps them navigate confines that might prove troublesome for other creatures of their size, yet sewer-dwelling sludge trolls are still careful to avoid attracting too much attention from those in positions of power in the city above.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Easily Misled","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The sludge troll gets a –4 circumstance penalty to their Perception DC against Deception checks.</p>"},{"n":"Regeneration 20 (Deactivated by Cold or Fire)","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>This monster regains the listed number of Hit Points each round at the beginning of its turn. Its Dying condition never increases beyond Dying 3 as long as its regeneration is active. However, if it takes damage of a type listed in the regeneration entry, its regeneration deactivates until the end of its next turn. Deactivate the regeneration before applying any damage of a listed type, since that damage might kill the monster by bringing it to Dying 4.</p></p>"},{"n":"Furious Spit","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The sludge troll takes cold or fire damage</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The sludge troll makes a sludge cough Strike against a random creature within their first range increment. If the troll is taking persistent fire damage, they attempt a DC 15 Flat to remove it.</p>"},{"n":"Aquatic Upsurge","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The sludge troll is hiding in water, and a creature that hasn't detected it is within 30 feet</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The sludge troll Swims, then Leaps at the end of its Swim action, landing on any solid surface adjacent to the water. Once the triggering creature is in the troll's first range increment, the sludge troll makes a sludge cough Strike against the creature with a +2 circumstance bonus to the attack roll.</p>"},{"n":"Blinding Sludge","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature that is critically hit by a sludge troll's sludge cough becomes Blinded by the toxic sludge until it uses an Interact action to clear its eyes.</p>"},{"n":"Flexible Limbs","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A sludge troll's limbs and torso are eerily flexible. They can clamber and splash through difficult terrain caused by swamp features with ease (treating such areas as non-difficult terrain) as well as move through areas as if they were one size category smaller with ease (Medium, for the typical sludge troll).</p>"},{"n":"Rend","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Claw</p>\n<p><p>A Rend entry lists a Strike the monster has.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster hit the same enemy with two consecutive Strikes of the listed type in the same round.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster automatically deals that Strike's damage again to the enemy.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/adventure-toolbox/soul-glutton":{"lang":["Common","Daemonic","Necril"],"flavor":"<p>In the mist-choked fens and smoke-clogged ruins of Abaddon dwell creatures made of solid vapor whose roiling bodies drift among the fogs and effluvia of that wasted realm. Among the most unsettling of those are soul gluttons: drifting, ravenous monstrosities whose bodies shift and pulse like mist, yet are clammy and all too solid to the touch. While a soul glutton's favored meal is (naturally) souls, they eagerly attack the living to feed upon their life essence, gulping down the souls as they perish.</p>\n<p>Soul gluttons are particularly delighted to be drawn to worlds within the Universe, for here the souls are of a much wider variety of flavors than the twisted, tormented shades that populate Abaddon. A soul glutton's eagerness to feed on worldly souls makes them notoriously difficult to control when conjured—but a promise to let them free upon the world after performing a relatively small service is often enough to secure their fleeting loyalty to a short-lived cause.</p><h2>Eaters of Souls</h2><p>These creatures have an unnerving affectation—they enjoy knowing those they feed upon, and before consuming a soul will often take time to converse with it while it still lives, or via talking corpse if the creature is already dead. Conversations with healthy creatures hold very little interest to soul gluttons, as life tends to distort the truths they enjoy hearing about. To a soul glutton, the greatest truths can be learned by reading the mind of a creature at the moment it passes away.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Soul Gorge","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","death","divine","manipulate"],"f":"","d":"<p>The soul glutton casts mind reading on a dying creature (or the corpse of a creature that died no more than 24 hours ago). If a dying creature fails its save, it must immediately attempt a recovery check. If the creature dies as a result of this check (or if the target was a fresh corpse), the soul glutton consumes a significant portion of the creature's soul and restores 30 healing Hit Points, gaining any Hit Points in excess of its maximum as temporary Hit Points that last for 24 hours. A creature slain in this way can be returned to life normally once the soul glutton that killed it has been slain, otherwise any attempt to restore life requires a DC 15 Flat for success.</p>"},{"n":"Soulscar","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The soul glutton's claw Strikes affect incorporeal creatures with the effects of a ghost touch property rune and scar the soul. The first time in a round that a creature is damaged by a soul glutton's claw Strike, it must succeed at a DC 30 Will save or become Frightened 2 (plus Doomed 1 on a critical failure).</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-2-castle-grounds/sovina-vosslund":{"lang":["Aklo","Common"],"lore":[["Scribing Lore",15]],"abilities":[{"n":"Spewing Honey","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When Sovina takes slashing damage, her wound spews rancid honey mixed with bile on adjacent creatures, dealing 2d10 poison damage (DC 22 basic Reflex save). A creature that fails this saving throw is also Slowed 1 for 1 round by the thick, sticky honey. Sovina loses her spewing honey and penanggalan bile abilities until the end of her next turn.</p>"},{"n":"Constrict","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>2d6+4 bludgeoning, DC 25 basic Fortitude</p><hr><p><p>The monster deals the listed amount of damage to any number of creatures Grabbed or Restrained by it. Each of those creatures can attempt a basic Fortitude save with the listed DC.</p></p>"},{"n":"Elongate Tongue","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The penanggalan's tongue extends, the membrane stretching and becoming translucent. Until the end of the turn, the penanggalan's proboscis tongue Strikes have a 10-foot reach, and any target is Off-Guard against the Strike unless it has a Perception DC of 25 or higher or the ability to precisely sense Invisible things.</p>"},{"n":"Penanggalan Bile","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["disease"],"f":"","d":"<p>Rest doesn't decrease the drained value from penanggalan bile</p>\n<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 22 Fortitude</p><hr><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> Drained 1 (1 week)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 2</strong> Drained 2 (1 week)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 3</strong> Drained 3 (1 week)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 4</strong> dead</p>"},{"n":"Ride Corpse","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>The penanggalan inserts their entrails into their humanoid body, allowing them to appear as and move about like a normal human. The body has 10 Hit Points and the same defenses as the penanggalan.</p>\n<p>When the body is destroyed, the penanggalan is ejected unharmed. The body becomes a corpse, and if it is neither controlled by the penanggalan nor stored in an alchemical vat, it decays as normal.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-5-the-dungeon/taxidermy-allosaurus":{"abilities":[{"n":"Scarecrow's Leer","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","emotion","fear","mental","occult","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>40 feet.</p>\n<p>The scarecrow's eyes flicker with an unnerving glow. A creature can't reduce its Frightened condition below 1 as long as it is in the aura's emanation. When a creature enters or starts its turn in the aura, it must attempt a DC 27 Will save. Living creatures made of flesh take a -2 circumstance penalty to this save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected and is then temporarily immune for 24 hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2 and is Fascinated by the scarecrow until the end of its next turn.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but Frightened 3.</p>"},{"n":"Baleful Glow","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","light","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The scarecrow's head bursts into ghostly, heatless flame that sheds bright light in a 20-foot emanation (and dim light to the next 20 feet). If the scarecrow uses this ability on the first round of combat, any creature that has not acted yet is startled and becomes Off-Guard against the scarecrow for 1 round. It can suppress the light by using this action again.</p>"},{"n":"Mundane Appearance","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate"],"f":"","d":"<p>Until it acts, the taxidermy allosaurus resembles an ordinary (if well-crafted) taxidermy creature. It has an automatic result of 43 on Deception checks and DCs to pass as an ordinary taxidermy display.</p>"},{"n":"Thrashing Tail","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["move"],"f":"","d":"<p>The taxidermy allosaurus smashes furniture and displays in the area with its tail, spreading the resulting rubble around. A 5-foot by 15-foot area of your choice that has at least one square adjacent to the creature becomes difficult terrain. If this area includes the stone tub, the acid splashes out and the difficult terrain becomes hazardous terrain as well that deals 1d6 acid damage.</p>"},{"n":"Toothsome Fear","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The taxidermy allosaurus's strikes deal an additional 2d6 mental damage to frightened creatures.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-6-the-private-halls/the-babies-are-so-hungry":{"description":"<p>The dreamwell plant shudders and pulses, emitting more potent odors before it bursts into swarms of ravenous ghostly spiders.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 33 Nature (master) to use the dreamwell plant's own blooms to soothe the ghostly spiders or DC 36 Religion (trained) to exorcise the ghostly spiders; three successes are required to disable the haunt completely</p>","reset":"<p>The haunt deactivates at the end of any round in which there are no Small or larger living creatures in the room, then resets automatically.</p>","routine":"<p>(2 actions) An immense swarm of thumb-sized spiders boil out of the plant's planter box and its bulbous trunk, filling the air with the nauseating sound of tens of thousands of tiny clawed feet scuttling everywhere. The haunt uses its first action to spray webbing through the area so that every creature in the room must succeed at a DC 30 Reflex save to avoid taking a –5-foot status penalty to their Speed. These penalties stack with multiple failed Reflex saves, and a character whose penalties reduce their Speed to 0 becomes Immobilized (Escape).</p><p>On its second action, the phantasmal spiders begin to bite and gnaw at living flesh with fangs that are suddenly both very real and very toxic. Every creature in the room takes 2d10 piercing and 2d10 poison damage (DC 30 basic Fortitude save).</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(master)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Dreamtime Bloom","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["incapacitation","mental","sleep"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A Small or larger living creature spends more than a minute in the room, or anyone attempts to move or damage the plant</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The dreamwell plant's flowers bloom and fill the air in area <strong>F14</strong> with soporific scents. All creatures in the room must attempt a DC 30 Will save with the following effects; the haunt then rolls initiative.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The character is unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The character takes a –1 status penalty to their initiative roll at the start of the following round.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> As success, but the character is also Slowed 1 from sudden tiredness. The affected character can attempt a new DC 30 Will save at the end of a round to recover from this condition; otherwise, it lasts for 1 minute.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The character falls Unconscious. If it's still unconscious (and alive) after 1 hour, it wakes up automatically.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-4-the-reception-halls/the-bastards-portrait":{"description":"<p>A mysterious portrait lies face down on the floor.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 31 Thievery (trained) to carefully dismantle the portrait without jostling it enough to trigger it or DC 33 Religion (expert) to exorcise the haunt; 3 successes are required to disable it.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(master)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"You Will Know My Name!","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental","occult","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature attempts to move the portrait</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Once someone flips the portrait over, they see a headless man portrayed in fine clothing, only to have the name \"VELICARN\" appear across the portrait in the form of bloody letters that run from the headless man's raw stump. If the triggering creature didn't attempt to look at the portrait, the painting wrenches and twists to present itself to the target, who must succeed at a DC 28 Will save to avoid becoming Frightened 1 (Frightened 2 on a critical failure). A moment later, a trio of shadowy forms that drip and splatter blood surge out from the portrait, leaving it a blank canvas. These shapes appear vaguely as headless humanoids made of red smoke and leave bloodstains wherever they touch, but they're otherwise treated as typical wraiths. The wraiths roll initiative and remain for 1 minute, after which they vanish, and the portrait becomes a blank but bloodstained canvas. The wraiths also vanish if someone disables the haunt before the duration expires.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-6-the-private-halls/the-mistake":{"lang":["Common","can't speak any language"],"notes":{"hp":"meant to live, void healing"},"abilities":[{"n":"Meant to Live","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>Whenever an unrisen would take damage from vitality energy, it instead heals half that number of Hit Points.</p>"},{"n":"Resurrection Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature with a prepared or spontaneous spell that can restore the dead to life (such as Breath of Life or Raise Dead) can expend an appropriate spell slot as a 2-action activity to destroy an unrisen within 30 feet. The attempt fails if the unrisen succeeds at a Will save against the creature's spell DC.</p>"},{"n":"Rise Again","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>As unrisen, but the heart-like Vile Remnant lodged within the creature's chest becomes momentarily visible as it's returned by this ability, allowing The Mistake to use Show the Horror.</p><hr><p>If the unrisen is reduced to 0 Hit Points by means other than fire damage, disintegration, or its resurrection vulnerability, it returns to unlife at the start of its next turn. It has 100 Hit Points and is Prone in the space in which it was destroyed. The unrisen can't be returned by this ability again for 1 hour.</p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Agonized Howl","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The unrisen howls in pain at its cursed existence. Creatures within a 30-foot emanation take 10d8 mental damage with a DC 32 basic Will save. The unrisen can't use Agonized Howl again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Awful Approach","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"once per 10 minutes","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per 10 minutes</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The unrisen reshapes its grotesque form to move swiftly. It Strides twice. Any living creature that can see the unrisen during this movement must succeed at a DC 29 Fortitude save or be Sickened 1 (or Sickened 2 on a critical failure); this is a mental and visual effect.</p>"},{"n":"Death Grip","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["curse","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The unrisen has a living creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The unrisen attempts to destroy its victim's life force so it shares in the unrisen's fate. The creature must succeed at a DC 32 Fortitude save or become Doomed 1. While the curse lasts, the creature regains only half as many HP from effects with both the healing and vitality traits; if it dies, any attempt to raise it from the dead causes it to return as an unrisen. The curse ends automatically if the creature's doomed value is reduced to 0.</p>"},{"n":"Show the Horror","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["curse","emotion","fear","mental","occult","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The Mistake has used rise again within the previous hour</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The Mistake grabs onto its chest and tugs it open, cracking ribs and tearing flesh to give a glimpse of the horrible Vile Remnant lodged inside of it. All creatures within 30 feet must attempt a DC 32 Will save with the following results. The Mistake can't Show the Horror again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unsettled by the sight and becomes Frightened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature becomes Frightened 2 and feels something shift in its own chest, as if a similarly shaped horror was within its own ribcage and twitching in delight at seeing its twin in another place. The affected creature can't reduce its frightened condition below 1 as long as The Mistake remains active and within 60 feet.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but Frightened 3, and the creature continues to feel that awful chest twitch any time thereafter it becomes frightened. The creature becomes Slowed 1 whenever it's frightened as it uses one of its actions to grip at its chest and worry about the implications of what might be inside of it; this curse has an unlimited duration.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-1-the-haunted-shore/the-sky-is-burning":{"description":"<p>Fires suddenly spring up along the rafters of the exposed roof as strange, monstrous shapes can be glimpsed in the sky above, which is suddenly filled with smoke.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 26 Survival (trained) to extinguish fires swiftly or DC 23 Religion (expert) to exorcise the haunt; two total successes are required to disable the haunt</p>","reset":"<p>The haunt deactivates and then resets after 1 round in which no living creatures are within the schoolhouse.</p>","routine":"<p>(4 actions) Ghostly figures flying in the skies above hurl bits of burning debris down into the three rooms of the schoolhouse below. The haunt uses its first three actions to target a random creature within the schoolhouse with a burning debris ranged Strike. A creature can't be targeted more than once per round—if there aren't enough targets for all 3 Strikes, the leftover Strike actions are wasted. On its fourth action, the haunt causes the fires in the building itself to flare up—all creatures inside the building take 2d8 fire damage (DC 21 basic Reflex save). These flare-ups are the most extreme in area <strong>A4a</strong>; creatures in areas <strong>A4b</strong> or <strong>A4c</strong> gain a +2 circumstance bonus on this Reflex save.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(expert)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"The Sky Breathes Smoke","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A living creature ends their turn inside the schoolroom</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Harrowing shrieks and frightening howls echo from the skies above as smoke plumes down into the rooms below. All creatures in the schoolhouse (areas <strong>A4a–A4c</strong>) must attempt a DC 21 Fortitude save to avoid becoming Sickened 1 from the smoke (Sickened 2 on a critical failure). The haunt then rolls initiative.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/they-grow-inside":{"description":"<p>When the bin is opened, it collapses, spilling out the infested corpse within.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 23 Thievery (expert) to reinforce the bin so it doesn't collapse (although if the PCs then search the remains, they still trigger the hazard), DC 27 Religion (master) to exorcise the haunt.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(expert)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Horrific Infestation","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":["curse","fear","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The seed bin is opened, or the body within is jostled or searched</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The body spills out and splits open, releasing an agonizing scream. All creatures in area <strong>C4</strong> must succeed at a DC 29 Will save or become Frightened 1 (Frightened 2 and Fleeing as long as they remain frightened on a critical failure). The ghoulish gardeners in area <strong>C7</strong> come to investigate soon thereafter. The creature who triggered the haunt must also succeed at a DC 29 Will save with the following effects as it takes a blast of spore-like seeds to the face.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected by the infestation.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature believes it inhaled some of the stuff, and becomes Sickened 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> As success, but the creature now fears that it's infected by the corrupt seeds, and imagines it can feel plants growing inside itself. This is all in the creature's mind, but for the next 8 hours, the sensation makes it Clumsy 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the clumsy condition has an unlimited duration.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-9-the-towers/trapped-arudora-soul":{"description":"<p>A ghostly image of one of the Arudoras floats in the middle of the room, arms folded over their chest as if reclining in a coffin, yet they hover vertically and are shrouded by ribbons of green smoke.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 41 Religion to exorcise the haunt, or one of the following checks depending on the Arudora; four total successes are required to disable the haunt.</p><p><strong>J3a (Jinnivere)</strong> DC 38 Nature to draw upon her love of horses</p><p><strong>J3b (Dazerien)</strong> DC 38 Intimidation to frighten the cruel murderer</p><p><strong>J3c (Ryhasphinea)</strong> DC 38 Deception to trick her into believing she's been forsaken by Urgathoa</p><p><strong>J3d (Augsten)</strong> DC 38 Diplomacy to explain that abuse of the servants shows weakness of character</p><p><strong>J3e (Alisendra)</strong> DC 38 Performance to give her one last entertainment</p><p><strong>J3f (Raudltz)</strong> DC 38 Society to help him come to terms with the shame he feels for his infidelities</p><p><strong>J3g (Irrokcis)</strong> DC 38 Occultism to use his own obscure theories against him to banish him</p>","reset":"<p>The haunt deactivates as soon as no living creatures are in the room, then immediately resets.</p>","routine":"<p>(1 action) On its initiative, the haunt transforms into a physical manifestation of that Arudora's personality and lashes out, dealing 9d6 untyped damage to all creatures in the room and within 30 feet of any open doorway outside the room. A PC can attempt a DC 34 basic saving throw against the damage. The type of damage and saving throw to defend against it depend on the Arudora, as does the additional condition imparted on a critical failure. Conditions imparted by this haunt are curses that have unlimited durations.</p><p><strong>J3a (Jinnivere)</strong> trampling horses (bludgeoning damage, Reflex save, Clumsy 1 on a critical failure)</p><p><strong>J3b (Dazerien)</strong> complex torture devices (piercing damage, Reflex save, Enfeebled 1 on a critical failure)</p><p><strong>J3c (Ryhasphinea)</strong> skull-faced spirits (spirit damage, Will save, Stupefied 1 on a critical failure)</p><p><strong>J3d (Augsten)</strong> barbed whip and scourge Strikes (slashing damage, Reflex save, Enfeebled 1 on a critical failure)</p><p><strong>J3e (Alisendra)</strong> shrieking spirits and discordant instruments (sonic damage, Fortitude save, stupefied 1 on a critical failure)</p><p><strong>J3f (Raudltz)</strong> ghostly fangs that chew into your thoughts (mental damage, Will save, Drained 1 on a critical failure)</p><p><strong>J3g (Irrokcis)</strong> decaying skeletal limbs (void damage, Fortitude save, drained 1 on a critical failure)</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(master) to notice that the eerie manifestation is dangerous; noticing the ghostly image of the Arudora in question floating in the middle of the room has a DC of 0</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Awaken","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A living creature ends their turn in the room</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The soul's eyes open, and it cries out in agony as its ghostly body twists and transforms into something monstrous. The triggering PC must succeed at a DC 34 Will save or become Frightened 2 (Frightened 3 and Fleeing for as long as they're frightened on a critical failure). The haunt then rolls initiative.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-8-the-vault/vault-bats":{"lang":["Aklo","telepathy 100 feet"],"notes":{"saves":"–1 status penalty to all saves vs. death effects"},"abilities":[{"n":"Bound to Bastardhall","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Unlike the typical phantom, these phantoms exist fully in the Ethereal Plane and can't shift back and forth between this plane and the Universe.</p>"},{"n":"–1 Status Penalty to All Saves vs. Death Effects","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Susceptible to Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Though phantoms aren't alive, neither are they undead, and they are uniquely vulnerable to the effects of death.</p>\n<p>A phantom whose Hit Points are reduced to 0 as a result of a death effect (such as from a spell like Execute) is immediately whisked away to the River of Souls, where their soul resumes the usual path to the afterlife.</p>"},{"n":"Phantom Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["spirit"],"f":"","d":"<p>Each time they make a Strike, a phantom can choose to deal spirit damage instead of the normal physical damage type.</p>"},{"n":"Unnerving Shriek","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","emotion","fear","mental","move","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The vault bat swoops downward, Flying once and shrieking with a soul-chilling cry as they do so. This movement doesn't trigger reactions. Any adjacent creature the vault bat passes during its Fly must attempt a DC 28 Will save or become Frightened 1 (or Frightened 3 on a critical failure).</p>"},{"n":"Grab Item","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The phantom beast attempts to Steal one item of up to 1 Bulk from a creature, even if the creature is in combat, though the object still must not be one that is actively in use. If they succeed, they carry the object along with them telekinetically.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-9-the-towers/velicarn-death-coach":{"lang":["Common","Necril"],"lore":[["Driving Lore",27]],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"abilities":[{"n":"Lifesense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>Lifesense allows a monster to sense the vital essence of living and undead creatures within the listed range. The sense can distinguish between the vitality energy animating living creatures and the void energy animating undead creatures, much as sight distinguishes colors.</p></p>"},{"n":"Aura of Doom","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","divine","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. A living creature that enters the area must succeed at a DC 33 Will save or gain the Doomed 1 condition. A critical failure increases this condition to Doomed 2. Regardless of the result of the saving throw, the creature is temporarily immune to Velicarn's aura of doom for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Bastard's Shame","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Velicarn harbors deep feelings of shame and anger about his heritage, and a PC can capitalize on this to insult him or to distract him as follows.</p>\n<p><strong>Insult Velicarn</strong> The PC attempts a DC 30 Intimidation check to Demoralize Velicarn. On a critical success, he's also Slowed 1 by his anger for 1 round. If he's also already Off-Guard as a result of being distracted, he takes 6d6 mental damage (DC 30 basic Will save) on a success (12d6 mental damage on a critical success).</p>\n<p><strong>Distract Velicarn</strong> The PC attempts a DC 28 Deception check to Feint Velicarn. On a critical success, he's also slowed 1 by his anger for 1 round. If he's also already slowed as a result of being insulted, he takes 6d6 mental damage (DC 30 basic Will save) on a success (12d6 mental damage on a critical success).</p>"},{"n":"Board Soul","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["death","divine"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> Velicarn reduces a creature to 0 HP with void damage</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Fragments of the creature's soul, manifesting as a vaguely spectral image of the creature, are pulled into the carriage and thereafter seen within, hammering silently on the windows while screaming to be rescued. A creature that dies while a fragment of its soul is trapped within the carriage can't become undead or be brought back to life until Velicarn is destroyed. A creature that's rescued before death is Stupefied 2 as long as part of its soul remains aboard the Black Coach.</p>"},{"n":"Coin's Return","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A PC can toss Velicarn's Cursed Coin, or the Fortune's Coin it becomes after it's uncursed, at the death coach as an Interact action whenever Velicarn is within 30 feet. Doing so causes the coin to vanish in a sudden flash. The coin is destroyed, and Velicarn must attempt a DC 35 Will save (DC 37 Will if the coin is no longer cursed) with the following results.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> Velicarn is Off-Guard until the start of his turn.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> Velicarn takes 5d6 spirit damage and is off-guard until the start of his turn.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> Velicarn takes 10d6 spirit damage, is off-guard until the end of the PC's next turn, and is Stunned 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> Velicarn takes 20d6 spirit damage, is off-guard for 1 minute, and is Stunned 3.</p>"},{"n":"Discorporation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>If Velicarn is reduced to 0 Hit Points, the Black Coach and the two fiendish horses that drive it vanish immediately, and Velicarn transforms into a dullahan armed with a +2 greater striking astral battle axe. He falls Prone in a space of his choice previously occupied by the death coach and is Slowed 1 for 1 round. As soon as he ends a round without the slowed condition, he transforms back into a death coach and regains 100 healing Hit Points. Only by destroying him in his dullahan form before this (typically 2 rounds, unless his slowed condition is extended) can he be truly put to rest.</p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Trample","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Large or smaller, incorporeal wheel, DC 36 basic Reflex</p><hr><p><p>The monster Strides up to double its Speed and can move through the spaces of creatures of the listed size, Trampling each creature whose space it enters. The monster can attempt to Trample the same creature only once in a single use of Trample. The monster deals the damage of the listed Strike, but trampled creatures can attempt a basic Reflex save at the listed DC (no damage on a critical success, half damage on a success, double damage on a critical failure).</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-9-the-towers/velicarn-dullahan":{"lang":["Common","Necril"],"gear":["Hatchet","Longsword","Battle Axe","Full Plate"],"notes":{"hp":"fast healing 5"},"flavor":"<p>Riding on a horse as black as night, the headless hunter known as the dullahan tracks down and takes the heads of those they deem unfit to continue living. When closing in for the kill, the dullahan first whispers their victim's name, then swiftly collects their prize, casting a pall of dread upon all who witness the grim execution.</p>\n<p>A dullahan manifests when a particularly violent warrior is beheaded and the warrior's soul stubbornly clings to material existence (or is refused entry to the afterlife). Most dullahans return to their former homelands, where they can exact vengeance on those they feel wronged them in life (or their living descendants). A dullahan's concept of justice is swift and merciless, and once they've selected a target, they're unwavering in their cause.</p>\n<p>Perhaps even more than revenge, a dullahan desires their own rotted head. An individual who wields the head of a dullahan is powerful indeed, for a dullahan will grudgingly serve such a master in the hopes of reclaiming their missing skull. Mighty fiends such as devils command dullahans to harvest souls or lead armies for them, while a mortal might use such an undead warrior to fulfill a personal vendetta. A dullahan won't hesitate to kill their liege and reclaim their head when the opportunity presents itself.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Lifesense 60 feet","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>Lifesense allows a monster to sense the vital essence of living and undead creatures within the listed range. The sense can distinguish between the vitality energy animating living creatures and the void energy animating undead creatures, much as sight distinguishes colors.</p></p>"},{"n":"Fast Healing 5","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A monster with this ability regains the given number of Hit Points each round at the beginning of its turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Bastard's Shame","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Velicarn harbors deep feelings of shame and anger about his heritage, and a PC can capitalize on this to insult him or to distract him as follows.</p>\n<p><strong>Insult Velicarn</strong> The PC attempts a DC 30 Intimidation check to Demoralize Velicarn. On a critical success, he's also Slowed 1 by his anger for 1 round. If he's also already Off-Guard as a result of being distracted, he takes 6d6 mental damage (DC 30 basic Will save) on a success (12d6 mental damage on a critical success).</p>\n<p><strong>Distract Velicarn</strong> The PC attempts a DC 28 Deception check to Feint Velicarn. On a critical success, he's also slowed 1 by his anger for 1 round. If he's also already slowed as a result of being insulted, he takes 6d6 mental damage (DC 30 basic Will save) on a success (12d6 mental damage on a critical success).</p>"},{"n":"Frightful Presence","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. DC 23 Will</p>\n<hr>\n<p><p>A creature that first enters the area must attempt a Will save.</p>\n<p>Regardless of the result of the saving throw, the creature is temporarily immune to this monster's Frightful Presence for 1 minute.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected by the presence.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 4.</p></p>"},{"n":"Reactive Strike","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Head Hunter","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Any slashing weapon gains the <em>Keen</em> rune while a dullahan wields it, and any hatchet they wield gains the <em>Returning</em> rune as well. If the dullahan kills a creature with a critical hit using a slashing weapon, the target is decapitated as though the dullahan had used Reap on the target.</p>"},{"n":"Reap","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The dullahan removes the head of a dead creature within reach. Each creature within the area of the dullahan's frightful presence must attempt a new save, even if it is temporarily immune.</p>"},{"n":"Summon Steed","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The dullahan summons a war horse with elite adjustments and the fiend and unholy traits. This steed remains until it is slain, the dullahan Dismisses this effect, or the dullahan Summons a Steed again.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-6-the-private-halls/velicarns-head":{"lang":["Common","Necril","can't speak any language"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"abilities":[{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Rupturing Shriek","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","curse","divine","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>Velicarn's head unleashes a horrific howl that causes blood to leak out of those who hear it and into his gaping open mouth. All creatures within 30 feet must succeed at a DC 28 Fortitude save or become Drained 1 (Drained 2 on a critical failure) as some of their blood runs from their bodies into Velicarn's maw. Creatures currently taking persistent bleed damage take a –2 circumstance penalty to this saving throw. Velicarn's head regains 5 healing Hit Points for every creature in the area, to a maximum of 25 healing Hit Points, and he can't use Rupturing Shriek again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Snatch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Velicarn's head can Fly at half Speed while he has a Medium or smaller creature Grabbed or Restrained in his talons, carrying that creature along with him.</p>"},{"n":"Swallow Whole","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["attack"],"f":"","d":"<p>Medium, 2d8+9 bludgeoning, Rupture 24</p><hr><p><p>The monster attempts to swallow a creature of the listed size or smaller that it has grabbed or restrained in its jaws or mouth. If a swallowed creature is of the maximum size listed, the monster can't use Swallow Whole again. If the creature is smaller than the maximum, the monster can usually swallow more creatures; the GM determines the maximum. The monster attempts an Athletics vs. Reflex DC check opposed by the grabbed creature's Reflex DC. If it succeeds, it swallows the creature. The monster's mouth or jaws no longer grab a creature it has swallowed, so the monster is free to use them to Strike or Grab once again. The monster can't attack creatures it has swallowed.</p>\n<p>A swallowed creature is Grabbed, is Slowed 1, and has to hold its breath or start suffocating. The swallowed creature takes the listed amount of damage when first swallowed and at the end of each of its turns while it's swallowed. If the victim Escapes this ability's grabbed condition, it exits through the monster's mouth. This frees any other creature grabbed in the monster's mouth or jaws. A swallowed creature can attack the monster that has swallowed it, but only with unarmed attacks or with weapons of light Bulk or less. The swallowing creature is Off-Guard against the attack. If the monster takes piercing or slashing damage equaling or exceeding the listed Rupture value from a single attack or spell, the swallowed creature cuts itself free. A creature that gets free by either Escaping or cutting itself free can immediately breathe and exits the swallowing monster's space.</p>\n<p>Effect: Engulf and Swallow Whole</p>\n<p>If the monster dies, a swallowed creature can be freed by creatures adjacent to the corpse if they spend a combined total of 3 actions cutting the monster open with a weapon or unarmed attack that deals piercing or slashing damage.</p></p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-6-the-private-halls/velicarns-head-haunt":{"description":"<p>A hideous mound of flesh, shattered bone, tangles of hair, and what appears to be gray matter lies in a glistening, 2-foot-tall heap on the floor, only to suddenly start piling up on itself to gradually form an immense, hateful head.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 30 Religion (expert) to exorcise the haunt; three successes area required to disable it</p>","reset":"<p>Velicarn's head collapses back into a 2-foot-high mound of gore at the end of any round in which no non-fiend living creatures are in the library. It then resets 1 minute later.</p>","routine":"<p>(1 action) On its turn, Velicarn's head continues to grow and expand as it becomes a monstrous distortion of his once-human appearance, with beady red eyes, sharp fangs, and two bat-like wings that unfurl from either side. The haunt screeches louder, and all creatures in the library take 4d6 persistent bleed damage as blood pours from their ears, eyes, and mouths (DC 28 basic Fortitude save). If Velicarn's head isn't disabled or destroyed by the end of the third round, it rips out of the ground and joins the fight as a horrific monstrosity—a daqqanoenyent. At this point, the haunt is automatically disabled, and the PCs must face this new threat. As a daqqanoenyent, Velicarn's head focuses its attacks on the PCs and ignores the sahkil (who, if still alive, titters and cackles in delight at this development). It pursues PCs relentlessly and fights until it's destroyed, which permanently destroys the hazard.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(master) to notice the mound of gore is haunted; noticing the mound itself has a DC of 0</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Awakened Horror","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["auditory","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A living non-fiend creature approaches within 15 feet of the remains of Velicarn's head</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The mound of gore shudders and quakes, then starts to pile up on itself, slowly transforming into an immense, screaming head. All living creatures in area <strong>F1</strong> must attempt a DC 28 Will save as the gurgling birth-shriek of the awakening horror tears through the room. Those who fail become Frightened 1 (Frightened 2 on a critical failure). The haunt then rolls initiative.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-3-the-cellars/vile-brew":{"description":"<p>The brewing equipment begins to shudder and shake, and thick foamy sludge starts to bubble up out of the various pipes, tuns, and barrels—sludge that contains blinking eyes, screaming mouths, and twitching fingers among its turbulent mass!</p>","disable":"<p>DC 28 Crafting (master) to swiftly dismantle key parts of the contraption containing the vile brew, or DC 31 Religion (expert) to exorcise the haunt; three total successes in all are needed to disable the haunt</p>","reset":"<p>The haunt deactivates at the start of any round in which no living creatures are in the room; it then reactivates 1 minute later.</p>","routine":"<p>(3 actions) On its initiative, the churning awfulness extrudes a long, slimy tendril of clotted blood, tangled hair, and jagged broken teeth. These long, whiplike lengths then make three vile lash Strikes on three randomly determined targets in the room. With their reach and ability to manifest from any part of the machinery, these Strikes can target any creature in the room. They only target a creature more than once if there's not enough targets to split their attention.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(expert)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Nightmare Recrudescence","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature attempts to use the brewing equipment, or at least one living creature spends 1 minute in the room</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The brewing equipment rattles to life as a hideous mash of what looks like clotted blood and body parts surges up and out of the machinery, pushing a nauseating stench into the room. All creatures in area <strong>C46</strong> must succeed at a DC 26 Fortitude save or become Sickened 1 (Sickened 2 on a critical failure). The haunt then rolls initiative.</p>"},{"n":"Flesh Fermentation","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 26 Fortitude</p>\n<p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 1</strong> 1d6 poison damage and Enfeebled 1 (1 round)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 2d6 poison damage and Enfeebled 2 (1 round)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 3</strong> 2d6 poison damage, enfeebled 2 (1 round), and Drained 1</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-5-the-dungeon/vink-cratched":{"lang":["Jotun","Common"],"notes":{"hp":"regeneration 20 (deactivated by cold or fire)"},"flavor":"<p>The lanky, greasy sludge troll always seems to look like they just slid out of a pool of noxious sewage or filthy swamp water, with their scaly hide dripping with oily fluids and crusty muck. While they possess the iconic underbite and tusks of the typical troll, their facial features are much more fishlike, with bulging eyes and fin-like ridges on their spine, brow, and forearms. In addition to possessing prodigious regenerative qualities, sludge trolls are immune to diseases, and as a result, they often wallow and lair in areas infested with sickness for additional defenses. Tunnels and sewage outflows are their favored places to lurk in ambush, particularly if these areas see lots of traffic from underworld denizens. Generally more stealthy and sneaky than the typical troll, a sludge troll roused to violence is as terrifying and impossible to ignore as any others of their kind.</p><h2>Sewer Dwellers</h2><p>While sludge trolls are often found dwelling in mires and bogs, they are also perfectly at home lurking in sewers and cisterns below urban areas. Their lanky limbs and ability to squeeze through smaller areas helps them navigate confines that might prove troublesome for other creatures of their size, yet sewer-dwelling sludge trolls are still careful to avoid attracting too much attention from those in positions of power in the city above.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Easily Misled","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The sludge troll gets a –4 circumstance penalty to their Perception DC against Deception checks.</p>"},{"n":"Regeneration 20 (Deactivated by Cold or Fire)","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>This monster regains the listed number of Hit Points each round at the beginning of its turn. Its Dying condition never increases beyond Dying 3 as long as its regeneration is active. However, if it takes damage of a type listed in the regeneration entry, its regeneration deactivates until the end of its next turn. Deactivate the regeneration before applying any damage of a listed type, since that damage might kill the monster by bringing it to Dying 4.</p></p>"},{"n":"Furious Spit","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The sludge troll takes cold or fire damage</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The sludge troll makes a sludge cough Strike against a random creature within their first range increment. If the troll is taking persistent fire damage, they attempt a DC 15 Flat to remove it.</p>"},{"n":"Aquatic Upsurge","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The sludge troll is hiding in water, and a creature that hasn't detected it is within 30 feet</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The sludge troll Swims, then Leaps at the end of its Swim action, landing on any solid surface adjacent to the water. Once the triggering creature is in the troll's first range increment, the sludge troll makes a sludge cough Strike against the creature with a +2 circumstance bonus to the attack roll.</p>"},{"n":"Blinding Sludge","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature that is critically hit by a sludge troll's sludge cough becomes Blinded by the toxic sludge until it uses an Interact action to clear its eyes.</p>"},{"n":"Goading Roar","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","emotion","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>Vink unleashes a gurgling, sputtering roar that sounds as if it issues from a throat partially clogged with mud. The sputtering sound isn't particularly frightening, but it compels other sludge trolls within 30 feet to grow more frenzied. All sludge trolls who hear Vink's Goading Roar become Quickened for 1 round, and may use the extra action to Stride or Swim.</p>"},{"n":"Rend","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Claw</p>\n<p><p>A Rend entry lists a Strike the monster has.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster hit the same enemy with two consecutive Strikes of the listed type in the same round.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster automatically deals that Strike's damage again to the enemy.</p></p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/adventure-toolbox/vinlach":{"lang":["Chthonian","Common","Diabolic","Empyrean","Requian","telepathy 100 feet"],"notes":{"ac":"all-around vision"},"flavor":"<p>The vinlach represents the overwhelming fear of birds—the sharp beaks, the razored talons, the incessantly fluttering wings, the alien all-seeing eyes, and the haunting shrill cry of the raptor when it spots defenseless prey. They appear as a tangled mass of flapping whippoorwill wings, constantly kicking up dander and dust, with a \"head\" composed of dozens of shrieking whippoorwill heads, all merged together in a twittering knot of meat and bone, eyes peering in all directions at once. The amalgamation stalks about on four long, stork-like legs tipped with murderous talons, and while their body of wings wouldn't seem to suggest it, they can use them to fly through the air with unnerving grace. Despite their bestial nature, vinlachs are quite intelligent and delight in tormenting sapient creatures, particularly those who have strong beliefs or faiths.</p><h2>Psychopomp Blasphemers</h2><p>While all sahkils descended from an initial group of blasphemous psychopomps who betrayed their calling and rebelled against Pharasma, the eerie vinlachs take this corruption one step further in their desecration of Pharasma's sacred animal—the whippoorwill. They take particular delight in haunting fallen churches of Pharasma or serving priests who have turned their back on the Lady of Graves for more sinister patrons.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Telepathy 100 Feet","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["aura","magical","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A monster with telepathy can communicate mentally with any creatures within the listed radius, as long as they share a language. This doesn't give any special access to their thoughts, and communicates no more information than normal speech would.</p></p>"},{"n":"Easy to Call","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A vinlach's level is considered 2 lower for the purpose of being conjured by the Binding Circle ritual (and potentially other rituals, at the GM's discretion), but it is always free to attack or leave instead of negotiate unless the primary caster's check is a critical success.</p>"},{"n":"All-Around Vision","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>This monster can see in all directions simultaneously and therefore can't be flanked.</p></p>"},{"n":"Soul-Wrenching Warbling","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["auditory","aura","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The vinlach's warbling voices are a mockery of the rhythmic sound of a flight of whippoorwills whistling away, sending chills into the hearts of those who hear them. A living creature that begins its turn within 30 feet of a vinlach must succeed at a DC 23 Will save or gain weakness 5 to spirit damage until the start of its next turn, as its soul aches to flee its body and retreat along the River of Souls to find peace from the unnerving cacophony.</p>\n<p>Effect: Soul-Wrenching Warbling</p>"},{"n":"Rend","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Talon</p>\n<p><p>A Rend entry lists a Strike the monster has.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster hit the same enemy with two consecutive Strikes of the listed type in the same round.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster automatically deals that Strike's damage again to the enemy.</p></p>"},{"n":"Skip Between","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["divine","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p>The sahkil moves from the Universe to the Ethereal Plane or vice-versa. While in the Ethereal Plane, they're unable to affect anything in the Universe, and they cannot be seen by beings or abilities in the Universe, unless these have an ability that can gaze into the Ethereal Plane. While on the Material Plane the inverse is true for anything on the Ethereal Plane. A summoned sahkil can't use Skip Between.</p>"},{"n":"Soul Shriek","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The vinlach's dozens of mouths suddenly shriek in terrifying unison, yet the sound created is not one that can be heard by ears—rather, it's heard by a living creature's soul, as the supernatural sound attempts to rip free the very animus of life. All creatures in a 40-foot cone take 9d6 spirit damage and must attempt a DC 26 Will save with the following results. The vinlach can't Soul Shriek again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature takes no damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage. If the creature can cast divine spells or has the holy trait, it's also Stupefied 1 for 1 minute.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes double damage. If the creature can cast divine spells or has the holy trait, it's also stupefied 1 for 24 hours.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/adventure-toolbox/voldrak":{"lang":["Aklo"],"flavor":"<p>It seems like every lake in Ustalav of any considerable size has its own legend about a lake monster, but in the case of Lake Laroba, these legends have been fact for as long as anyone can remember. The lake's denizen, Voldrak, was only rarely sighted in the days before the curse, despite entire expeditions of hopeful monster-watchers spending extensive hours on the lake.</p>\n<p>Now, like all other creatures swept up by the Bastardhall Curse, Voldrak has grown worse. The lake monster torments anyone who dares ply the waters for too long. He generally prefers to frighten or maim rather than kill, for this new Voldrak enjoys the knowledge that his horrific legend might spread.</p>\n<p>Voldrak is a sleek, plesiosaur-like serpent, with four clawed flippers, deep-red eyes, and a long tail that ends in a single long rudder-like fin. His teeth are long and terrible and protrude over his lips even when his mouth is closed.</p><h2>Voldrak's Fate</h2><p>Before the curse, Voldrak was an elusive Water Orm regarded by the Arudoras almost as a mascot or pet to such an extent that they worked his image into their family crest. After the curse, though, Voldrak became a much more aggressive and dangerous monster. While he's unlikely to survive the events of Bastion of Blasphemies, Lake Laroba is deep, and who's to say the lake monster's ghost doesn't survive in its depths after the PCs return home?</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Frightful Presence","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>60 feet (360 underwater). Will</p><hr><p><p>A creature that first enters the area must attempt a Will save.</p>\n<p>Regardless of the result of the saving throw, the creature is temporarily immune to this monster's Frightful Presence for 1 minute.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected by the presence.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 4.</p></p>"},{"n":"Dread Gaze","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["emotion","fear","incapacitation","mental","occult","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>Voldrak fixes his gaze upon a creature he can see within 60 feet. His eyes glow with blue light, then begin to pulse, compelling the creature he views to remain motionless with fear. The target must attempt a DC 36 Will save with the following effects. Voldrak can't use Dread Gaze again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected and is temporarily immune to Dread Gaze for 24 hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature becomes Frightened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature increases the value of its frightened condition by 1 (or becomes frightened 1 if not yet frightened). As long as the creature remains frightened, it is Slowed 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but as long as the creature remains frightened, it is Paralyzed.</p>"},{"n":"Maimed But Alive","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>If Voldrak would kill a creature with damage from his bite or tail Strike, he can opt instead to reduce the creature to 1 Hit Point and maim it, causing the creature to become permanently Enfeebled 3 or Clumsy 3 (Voldrak's choice) from the horrific wound.</p>"},{"n":"Toxic Spit","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["occult","poison"],"f":"","d":"<p>Voldrak spews a 60-foot line of toxic sludge, dealing 8d6 poison damage to all creatures in the area (DC 36 basic Fortitude save).</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-4-the-reception-halls/what-all-heroes-fear":{"description":"<p>The depictions of heroic deeds transform as the lights dim, becoming testaments to cowardice and failure.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 25 Intimidation to push back against the haunt with your own memories of bravery or DC 28 Religion to exorcise the haunt; three successes are required to disable it.</p>","reset":"<p>The haunt deactivates at the start of any round in which there are no living humanoids in the room, then resets 1 minute later.</p>","routine":"<p>(2 actions; emotion, fear, mental) On its initiative, the haunt's first action is to randomly select one humanoid in the room. All of the depictions of Eragayl in the artwork transform to depict that humanoid, who becomes the focus of the haunt. On its second action, the haunt's depictions animate, showing the targeted humanoid as they are slowly destroyed by what they fear most. All humanoids in the room must make a DC 24 Will save, with the target taking a –2 status penalty to the save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes 2d6+1 mental damage from the conviction that they'll be remembered as a coward and a failure.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> As success, but 4d6+2 mental damage, and the creature increases its frightened condition value by 1 to a maximum of Frightened 4.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but 8d6+4 mental damage. If the creature is already frightened 4, they become Paralyzed with fear until the start of the haunt's next turn.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(expert)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Your Legacies are Failures","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A humanoid ends their turn in the room</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The room's built-in light sources lower to dim illumination (although any light sources carried by PCs are unaffected), and the depictions of the heroics in the dioramas and statues transform into depictions of fear and cowardice. All humanoids in the room must make a DC 24 Will save to avoid becoming Frightened 1 (Frightened 2 on a critical failure). The haunt then rolls initiative.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-1-the-haunted-shore/wrathful-armor":{"notes":{"ac":"(13 when broken); construct armor"},"flavor":"<p>Suits of animated armor see use both as guardians and as training partners in high-end martial academies able to afford the extravagance. Often, a warrior's old suit of armor can be turned into animated armor once it accrues too much battle damage to provide adequate protection. They're found most often in wizard laboratories and ancient dungeons.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Granted a semblance of life through the use of rituals or other strange magic, animated objects take many forms and serve a variety of uses. A few examples of typical animated objects are listed below. Many of these creatures serve as guardians, surprising unsuspecting adventurers when they suddenly attack. Others serve as idle distractions for the exceptionally rich, simple servants created to handle odd jobs, and the like.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Construct Armor (Hardness 9)","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Like normal objects, an animated armor has Hardness. This Hardness reduces any damage it takes by an amount equal to the Hardness. Once an animated armor is reduced to less than half its Hit Points, or immediately upon being damaged by a critical hit, its construct armor breaks and its Armor Class is reduced to 13.</p>"},{"n":"Soul Bolstering","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a suit of wrathful armor is destroyed, it falls to pieces and the soul energy that animated it redistributes to the remaining active suits, trailing prismatic energy and cries of pain as it goes. The first time this occurs, the soul flows into the three remaining suits, granting each suit 4 temporary HP. The second time this occurs, the soul flows into the two remaining suits, granting each 6 temporary HP. The third time this occurs, the soul flows to the one remaining suit, granting it 12 temporary HP. These temporary HP persist until they're lost or until they're replaced by a greater value of temporary HP.</p>"},{"n":"Tetanus","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature that takes damage from a wrathful armor's glaive or gauntlet strike must succeed at a DC 14 Fortitude save to resist contracting tetanus</p><hr><p><strong>Tetanus</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Onset</strong> 10 days</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 1</strong> Clumsy 1 (1 week)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 2</strong> Clumsy 2 and can't speak (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 3</strong> Paralyzed with spasms (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 4</strong> death</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-9-the-towers/zaurgaulus":{"lang":["Common","Draconic","Telepathy 90 feet, Truespeech"],"notes":{"saves":"+2 status to all saves vs. occult","per":"fearsense (imprecise) 60 feet"},"flavor":"<p>Fear is one of the most powerful emotions, and despair dragons are masters of channeling those feelings of terror and hopelessness for their own benefit. As with other occult dragons, despair dragons are driven by an innate compulsion, in their case, the desire to strike terror in others. Despair dragons tend to settle near settlements, so they have a source of fear to draw from. The most common items in despair dragon hoards are scrolls, tomes, and relics that serve as research material. Through these, despair dragons learn of local folklore, urban legends, and more to better haunt their targets.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Telepathy 90 feet","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["aura","magical","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A monster with telepathy can communicate mentally with any creatures within the listed radius, as long as they share a language. This doesn't give any special access to their thoughts, and communicates no more information than normal speech would.</p></p>"},{"n":"Fearsense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["emotion","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The dragon senses all creatures with the frightened condition at the listed range.</p>"},{"n":"+2 Status to All Saves vs. Occult","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Consume Fear","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 60 feet loses the Frightened condition</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The dragon feasts upon the fear that leaves the triggering creature's body, gaining 6d8 temporary Hit Points that last for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Frightful Presence","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>60 feet. DC 37 Will</p><hr><p><p>A creature that first enters the area must attempt a Will save.</p>\n<p>Regardless of the result of the saving throw, the creature is temporarily immune to this monster's Frightful Presence for 1 minute.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected by the presence.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 4.</p></p>"},{"n":"Unbidden Thoughts","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The dragon is critically hit with a weapon or unarmed attack</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The attacker's mind fills with visions of their worst fears that overwhelm their senses, and they must choose one of the following results: either the triggering attack becomes a normal success, or the critical hit is unaffected but the triggering creature becomes Frightened 2.</p>"},{"n":"Draconic Frenzy","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The dragon makes two claw Strikes and one tail Strike in any order.</p>"},{"n":"Draconic Momentum","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The dragon recharges their Shrieking Breath whenever they score a critical hit with a Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Shrieking Breath","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["mental","occult","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p>The dragon lets out a cacophonous sound made of every scream the dragon has drawn from a terrified enemy, dealing 15d6 sonic damage in a 40-foot cone (DC 37 basic Will save). Creatures who fail their Will save must spend the first action of their next turn doing nothing but screaming. The dragon can't use Shrieking Breath again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Tongue Decoy","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["manipulate","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The despair dragon inflates several bladders at the end of its tongue to create the basic form of a creature. This process takes 1 minute to complete, during which the dragon is Off-Guard. If the dragon stops or is interrupted in this process, the bladders deflate, and the dragon must start over. Once the process is complete, the dragon can maintain the inflated bladders indefinitely, and can Dismiss to deflate the bladders and retract its tongue instantly.</p>\n<p>The inflated tongue takes the basic form of an animal or humanoid and can be inflated to be either Small or Medium. The form resembles the general silhouette of a creature, though closer inspection and success at a DC 33 Perception check can determine the true nature of the tongue. While inflated, the dragon can send its voice through the decoy, though keeping its tongue inflated makes it difficult to speak, causing the dragon to take a –4 circumstance penalty to any checks related to speaking, such as Deception checks to Lie.</p>\n<p>The dragon's tongue can extend up to 90 feet from the dragon's body and it can fully extend its tongue as part of the process to inflate the bladders. The dragon can move the inflated part of its tongue up to 15 feet at a time with an action, which has the concentrate, manipulate, and move traits. While extended, the inflated end of the tongue occupies space as a creature of the appropriate size, but the rest of the tongue doesn't impede or block movement in any way. The dragon's scent functions through cilia at the end of the tongue, but otherwise the dragon has no means of knowing what's near its tongue.</p>\n<p>Attacking the tongue is the same as attacking the dragon, except that the tongue is always Off-Guard. If the tongue takes any damage, it immediately deflates and remains out. The dragon remains off-guard as long as its tongue is out, but the dragon can retract its tongue with two consecutive Interact actions. If the tongue takes damage, the dragon can't use its tongue decoy again for 1 day.</p>"}]},"bastion-of-blasphemies-bestiary/chapter-2-castle-grounds/zizminarial":{"lang":["Aklo","Chthonian","Common","Daemonic","Diabolic","Draconic","Requian","telepathy 100 feet"],"notes":{"saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. magic","per":"smell prayers (precise) 60 feet"},"abilities":[{"n":"Smell Prayers","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Zizminarial is aware of divine magical items and active divine spells as a precise sense but cannot sense other types of magic at all.</p>"},{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Magic","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Ignite Soul","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["fear","fire","occult"],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> Zizminarial is in area <strong>B12</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Zizminarial causes the flame of a burning candle to suddenly flare up. The soul within shrieks in pain as the fire lances out to burn a creature in area <strong>B12</strong>, who takes 4d6 fire damage (DC 24 basic Reflex save; Frightened 1 on a failure or critical failure). The candle then burns out.</p>"},{"n":"Residual Force","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","force"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The venedaemon's most recent action was to cast a spell</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> Fading runes cling to the venedaemon's tentacles. The venedaemon makes a tentacle Strike that has a reach of 20 feet and deals 2d4 additional force damage.</p>"},{"n":"Soul Spell","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>If Zizminarial ingests a soul gem from a cacodaemon, he can recover an expended spell slot instead of gaining fast healing. The spell slot's rank can be no higher than half the level of the creature whose soul was consumed, rounded up.</p>\n<p>Zizminarial can ingest a soul from one of the 22 candles burning in this room to regain a spell slot of any level; he cannot use a soul candle to gain fast healing, though.</p>"},{"n":"Twisted Whispers","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","auditory","concentrate","linguistic","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The venedaemon whispers to a creature within 15 feet, which must succeed at a DC 24 Will save or be Stupefied 2 for 1 minute (or Stupefied 3 on a critical failure).</p>\n<p>Regardless of the results of the save, the creature is immune to Twisted Whispers for 24 hours.</p>"}]}});
